/* TrainTanmay Chemistry – AQA 7405 3.3 Organic chemistry: topics, notes, key terms, flashcards, equations, quiz questions and PMT packs.
   Notes are AI-written summaries to check against the AQA specification and a textbook. */
(function(){
const S=window.SPEC;
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.1","mod":3,"as":true,"title":"Introduction to organic chemistry","short":"Nomenclature, formulae, reaction mechanisms and isomerism.","spec":["Molecular, empirical, structural, displayed and skeletal formulae; homologous series and functional groups","IUPAC naming of organic compounds with up to six carbons in the main chain","Reaction mechanisms with curly arrows; free radicals","Structural isomerism: chain, position and functional group isomers","Stereoisomerism: E–Z isomerism from restricted rotation about C=C; CIP priority rules"],"notes":"\n<h2>What organic chemistry is</h2>\n<p>Organic chemistry is the chemistry of carbon compounds. Carbon forms four covalent bonds and can bond to itself in chains and rings, so there are millions of organic compounds. To make sense of them, we group compounds into <strong>homologous series</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>homologous series</strong> is a series of organic compounds with the same functional group and the same general formula, in which each member differs from the next by a <strong>CH₂</strong> group. Members have similar chemical properties and a gradual trend in physical properties (boiling point rises with chain length).</li>\n<li>A <strong>functional group</strong> is the part of a molecule (an atom or group of atoms) responsible for its characteristic chemical reactions.</li>\n<li>Hydrocarbons are <strong>saturated</strong> (only single C–C bonds, like alkanes) or <strong>unsaturated</strong> (containing C=C double bonds, like alkenes).</li>\n<li><strong>Aliphatic</strong>: carbon atoms joined in straight or branched chains, or non-aromatic rings. <strong>Alicyclic</strong>: aliphatic atoms arranged in a ring (cyclohexane). <strong>Aromatic</strong>: containing a benzene ring.</li>\n<li>An <strong>alkyl group</strong> (R) is an alkane with one hydrogen removed: methyl CH₃–, ethyl C₂H₅–, propyl C₃H₇–.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Functional groups to recognise</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Series</th><th>Group</th><th>Prefix / suffix</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>alkane</td><td>C–C only</td><td>-ane</td><td>propane</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alkene</td><td>C=C</td><td>-ene</td><td>but-2-ene</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alcohol</td><td>–OH</td><td>-ol (hydroxy-)</td><td>propan-1-ol</td></tr>\n<tr><td>halogenoalkane</td><td>–F, –Cl, –Br, –I</td><td>fluoro-, chloro-, bromo-, iodo-</td><td>2-bromopropane</td></tr>\n<tr><td>aldehyde</td><td>–CHO</td><td>-al</td><td>ethanal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ketone</td><td>C–CO–C</td><td>-one</td><td>propanone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>carboxylic acid</td><td>–COOH</td><td>-oic acid</td><td>ethanoic acid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ester</td><td>–COO–</td><td>-yl -oate</td><td>methyl ethanoate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>acyl chloride</td><td>–COCl</td><td>-oyl chloride</td><td>ethanoyl chloride</td></tr>\n<tr><td>amine</td><td>–NH₂</td><td>-amine (amino-)</td><td>methylamine</td></tr>\n<tr><td>nitrile</td><td>–C≡N</td><td>-nitrile</td><td>propanenitrile</td></tr>\n<tr><td>amide</td><td>–CONH₂</td><td>-amide</td><td>ethanamide</td></tr>\n<tr><td>arene</td><td>benzene ring</td><td>phenyl-, -benzene</td><td>methylbenzene</td></tr></table></div>\n\n<h2>Naming (IUPAC nomenclature)</h2>\n<p>Stems give the number of carbons in the longest chain: meth (1), eth (2), prop (3), but (4), pent (5), hex (6), hept (7), oct (8), non (9), dec (10).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the <strong>longest carbon chain</strong> containing the functional group; it gives the stem.</li>\n<li>Number the chain from the end that gives the functional group (or the first branch) the <strong>lowest number</strong>.</li>\n<li>Name the side chains and halogens as prefixes, in <strong>alphabetical order</strong>, each with its position number. Use di-, tri-, tetra- for repeats (these don't count for alphabetical order).</li>\n<li>Numbers are separated by commas; numbers and words by hyphens.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked examples.</strong></p>\n<p>CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃: longest chain 4 C, methyl on carbon 2: <strong>2-methylbutane</strong>.</p>\n<p>CH₃CHBrCH(CH₃)CH₃: 4 C chain, Br on C2, methyl on C3: <strong>2-bromo-3-methylbutane</strong>.</p>\n<p>CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃: <strong>but-1-ene</strong>. CH₃CH(OH)CH₃: <strong>propan-2-ol</strong>.</p></div>\n\n<h2>Types of formula</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>General formula</strong>: the simplest algebraic formula for a series, e.g. alkanes C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>, alkenes C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>, alcohols C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+1</sub>OH.</li>\n<li><strong>Molecular formula</strong>: the actual number of atoms of each element, e.g. C₄H₁₀.</li>\n<li><strong>Empirical formula</strong>: the simplest whole-number ratio, e.g. C₂H₅.</li>\n<li><strong>Structural formula</strong>: the minimum detail showing the arrangement of atoms, e.g. CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃.</li>\n<li><strong>Displayed formula</strong>: every atom and every bond drawn.</li>\n<li><strong>Skeletal formula</strong>: carbon chains drawn as zigzag lines with each corner and end a carbon; hydrogens on carbon are not shown, but functional groups are. Benzene is a hexagon with a circle.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Isomerism</h2>\n<p><strong>Structural isomers</strong> are compounds with the same molecular formula but different structural formulae. Three kinds:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chain</strong> isomers: different carbon skeleton (butane and 2-methylpropane, both C₄H₁₀).</li>\n<li><strong>Position</strong> isomers: functional group in a different place (propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol).</li>\n<li><strong>Functional group</strong> isomers: different functional group (propanal and propanone, both C₃H₆O).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stereoisomers (same structural formula, different arrangement in space) are in 4.1.3 (E/Z) and 6.2.2 (optical).</p>\n\n<h2>Breaking bonds and drawing mechanisms</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Homolytic fission</strong>: each bonding atom receives <strong>one electron</strong> from the bonded pair, forming two <strong>radicals</strong>. A <strong>radical</strong> is a species with an <strong>unpaired electron</strong>, shown with a dot: Cl•.</li>\n<li><strong>Heterolytic fission</strong>: one bonding atom receives <strong>both electrons</strong>, forming a cation and an anion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>curly arrow</strong> shows the movement of an <strong>electron pair</strong>. It starts at a lone pair or at the middle of a covalent bond, and its head points to where the pair ends up (an atom, forming a new bond, or between two atoms). Getting the start and end exactly right is where mechanism marks are won and lost.</p>\n<h3>Types of reaction</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Addition</strong>: two reactants join to make one product (alkenes).</li>\n<li><strong>Substitution</strong>: an atom or group is replaced by another (halogenoalkanes, alkanes).</li>\n<li><strong>Elimination</strong>: a small molecule (H₂O, HX) is removed from a molecule, forming a double bond (dehydration of alcohols).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mechanisms are also classified by the attacking species: <strong>nucleophile</strong> (electron-pair donor), <strong>electrophile</strong> (electron-pair acceptor) or <strong>radical</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> in skeletal formulae, every line end and every corner is a carbon. Count them carefully, and remember that a skeletal formula doesn't show the H atoms on carbon, but does show H on O or N (–OH, –NH₂).</p></blockquote>\n\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Draw and name all the structural isomers of C₅H₁₂.</p>\n<p>Three chain isomers: <strong>pentane</strong> CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃; <strong>2-methylbutane</strong> CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃; <strong>2,2-dimethylpropane</strong> C(CH₃)₄.</p>\n<p>(3-methylbutane is the same as 2-methylbutane numbered from the wrong end, and 2-ethylpropane is just pentane drawn differently.)</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Name CH₃CH₂CH(Cl)CH₃.</summary><div><p>2-chlorobutane.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What is the general formula of the alcohols?</summary><div><p>C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+1</sub>OH</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What type of fission forms radicals, and what is a radical?</summary><div><p>Homolytic fission; a radical is a species with an unpaired electron.</p></div></details>","terms":[["Homologous series","A series of organic compounds with the same functional group and general formula, each member differing by CH₂."],["Functional group","The part of an organic molecule responsible for its chemical reactions."],["Structural isomers","Compounds with the same molecular formula but different structural formulae."],["Radical","A species with an unpaired electron."],["Homolytic fission","Bond breaking in which each bonding atom receives one electron from the bonded pair, forming two radicals."],["Heterolytic fission","Bond breaking in which one bonding atom receives both electrons from the bonded pair."],["General formula","The simplest algebraic formula of a member of a homologous series, e.g. CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ for alkanes."],["Displayed formula","Shows the relative positioning of all atoms and every bond in a molecule."],["Skeletal formula","Shows the carbon skeleton as lines, with H atoms on carbons removed and functional groups shown."],["Structural formula","The minimal detail that shows the arrangement of atoms, e.g. CH₃CH₂CH₂OH."],["Saturated","Contains only single carbon–carbon bonds."],["Unsaturated","Contains at least one carbon–carbon multiple bond."],["Aliphatic","Carbon atoms joined in straight or branched chains, or non-aromatic rings."],["Alicyclic","Carbon atoms joined in a ring that is not aromatic."],["Aromatic","Contains a benzene ring."],["Curly arrow","Shows the movement of an electron pair, either breaking or forming a covalent bond."]],"qa":[["Stems for 1 to 6 carbon atoms","meth-, eth-, prop-, but-, pent-, hex-."],["Suffix or prefix for: alcohol, aldehyde, ketone, carboxylic acid","-ol, -al, -one, -oic acid."],["Prefix for halogen substituents","fluoro-, chloro-, bromo-, iodo-."],["Types of structural isomer","Chain, position and functional group isomers."],["Products of homolytic fission","Two radicals, each with one electron from the bond."],["Products of heterolytic fission","A cation and an anion: one atom takes both bonding electrons."],["Reaction types in organic chemistry","Addition, substitution and elimination."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Introduction to Organic Chemistry","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Detailed/3.1.%20Introduction%20to%20Organic%20Chemistry.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Definitions, Functional Groups, Nomenclature, Formulae and Yield","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.1.%20Definitions,%20Functional%20Groups,%20Nomenclature,%20Formulae%20and%20Yield.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Hybridisation in Carbon and Bond Formation","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.1.%20Hybridisation%20in%20Carbon%20and%20Bond%20Formation.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Introduction to Organic Chemistry","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Mind-Maps/3.1.%20Introduction%20to%20Organic%20Chemistry.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.2","mod":3,"as":true,"title":"Alkanes","short":"Fractional distillation, cracking, combustion and chlorination.","spec":["Fractional distillation of crude oil","Thermal and catalytic cracking: conditions and products","Combustion of alkanes; pollutants (CO, NOₓ, SO₂, unburned hydrocarbons, carbon particulates) and catalytic converters","Chlorination of alkanes by free-radical substitution in UV light: initiation, propagation and termination","Chlorine radicals and the breakdown of ozone by CFCs"],"notes":"\n<h2>Alkanes</h2>\n<p>Alkanes are <strong>saturated hydrocarbons</strong>, general formula C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>. Cycloalkanes are C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>. Each carbon forms four <strong>σ-bonds</strong> (sigma bonds): a σ-bond is formed by the <strong>direct, head-on overlap</strong> of orbitals between the bonding atoms. Free rotation is possible about a σ-bond. Each carbon has four bonding pairs, so the shape around it is <strong>tetrahedral</strong>, bond angle <strong>109.5°</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Boiling points</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chain length</strong>: longer chains have more electrons and more surface contact between molecules, so stronger <strong>London forces</strong> and higher boiling points.</li>\n<li><strong>Branching</strong>: branched isomers have lower boiling points. Branches stop molecules packing closely, so there are fewer points of surface contact and weaker London forces. Pentane boils at 36 °C, 2,2-dimethylpropane at 10 °C.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Reactivity</h3>\n<p>Alkanes are fairly unreactive: C–C and C–H bonds are strong (high bond enthalpy) and essentially <strong>non-polar</strong>, so they don't attract nucleophiles or electrophiles.</p>\n\n<h2>Combustion</h2>\n<p>Alkanes are used as fuels because their combustion is very exothermic.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Complete: C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O<br>Incomplete: C₃H₈ + 3½O₂ → 3CO + 4H₂O (or C, soot)</div>\n<p>Incomplete combustion happens when the oxygen supply is limited. It produces <strong>carbon monoxide</strong>, a toxic gas that binds to haemoglobin and stops blood carrying oxygen, and soot (carbon particulates), which can cause respiratory problems.</p>\n\n<h2>Radical substitution with halogens</h2>\n<p>In the presence of <strong>ultraviolet light</strong>, alkanes react with chlorine or bromine. A hydrogen atom is substituted by a halogen atom.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₄ + Br₂ → CH₃Br + HBr &nbsp;&nbsp;(UV light)</div>\n<p>The mechanism is <strong>radical substitution</strong>, in three stages:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Initiation</strong>: UV light breaks the Br–Br bond by homolytic fission, forming two radicals.<div class=\"rxn\">Br₂ → 2Br•</div></li>\n<li><strong>Propagation</strong>: a radical reacts with a molecule to make a new radical. The two steps repeat as a chain reaction.<div class=\"rxn\">CH₄ + Br• → •CH₃ + HBr<br>•CH₃ + Br₂ → CH₃Br + Br•</div></li>\n<li><strong>Termination</strong>: two radicals join, removing radicals and stopping the chain.<div class=\"rxn\">Br• + Br• → Br₂<br>•CH₃ + Br• → CH₃Br<br>•CH₃ + •CH₃ → C₂H₆</div></li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Limitations in synthesis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Further substitution</strong>: the product CH₃Br can react with another Br•, giving CH₂Br₂, CHBr₃ and CBr₄. Using excess alkane makes mono-substitution more likely.</li>\n<li><strong>Substitution at different positions</strong> in longer chains: propane gives a mixture of 1-bromopropane and 2-bromopropane.</li>\n<li>Termination can give longer-chain products (like C₂H₆ above).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So radical substitution gives a <strong>mixture of products</strong>, which have to be separated, and the yield of any one product is low.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> in propagation equations, check each step has one radical on each side, and that the two steps add up to the overall equation. Draw the radical dot on the atom that has the unpaired electron (•CH₃, not CH₃•, is the clearest).</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Alkanes as fuels: more detail</h2>\n<p>Crude oil is separated by fractional distillation into fractions of alkanes with similar boiling points. Shorter alkanes are in greater demand as fuels; longer ones are cracked into shorter alkanes and alkenes. The combustion of alkanes releases CO₂, a greenhouse gas, and incomplete combustion releases CO and soot. Sulfur impurities produce SO₂, which causes acid rain; car engines also produce NO<sub>x</sub> from nitrogen and oxygen in the air.</p>\n<h2>Writing combustion equations</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Write equations for the complete and incomplete (forming CO) combustion of hexane.</p>\n<p>Complete: C₆H₁₄ + 9½O₂ → 6CO₂ + 7H₂O (or 2C₆H₁₄ + 19O₂ → 12CO₂ + 14H₂O).</p>\n<p>Incomplete: C₆H₁₄ + 6½O₂ → 6CO + 7H₂O.</p></div>\n<h2>Radical substitution: more practice</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Write the mechanism for the formation of chloroethane from ethane.</p>\n<p>Initiation: Cl₂ → 2Cl• (UV).</p>\n<p>Propagation: C₂H₆ + Cl• → •C₂H₅ + HCl; •C₂H₅ + Cl₂ → C₂H₅Cl + Cl•.</p>\n<p>Termination: •C₂H₅ + Cl• → C₂H₅Cl; 2•C₂H₅ → C₄H₁₀; 2Cl• → Cl₂.</p></div>\n<p>Isomers from substitution: monochlorination of butane gives 1-chlorobutane and 2-chlorobutane, because H atoms on the end carbons and on the middle carbons are in different environments. Count the number of different H environments to predict how many mono-substituted isomers form.</p>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How many mono-brominated isomers can form from 2-methylpropane?</summary><div><p>Two: 1-bromo-2-methylpropane (substitution on a CH₃) and 2-bromo-2-methylpropane (on the central CH).</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why are alkanes unreactive towards most reagents?</summary><div><p>The C–C and C–H bonds are strong (high bond enthalpy) and essentially non-polar, so they don’t attract nucleophiles or electrophiles.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Explain why pentane has a higher boiling point than 2,2-dimethylpropane.</summary><div><p>Pentane is unbranched, so its molecules have more surface contact and stronger London forces, needing more energy to overcome.</p></div></details>\n<h2>More to try</h2><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the termination step that forms butane during the chlorination of ethane.</summary><div><p>•C₂H₅ + •C₂H₅ → C₄H₁₀</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is carbon monoxide toxic?</summary><div><p>It binds to haemoglobin (more strongly than oxygen), so the blood carries less oxygen.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Fractional distillation and cracking</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Crude oil is heated and the vapour rises up a <strong>fractionating column</strong> that is hot at the bottom and cooler at the top. Fractions condense at different heights according to their boiling points, which depend on chain length (longer chains, more van der Waals forces, higher boiling point).</li>\n<li><strong>Cracking</strong> breaks C–C bonds in long alkanes to make shorter, more useful alkanes and alkenes.</li>\n<li><strong>Thermal cracking</strong>: high pressure (up to 7000 kPa) and high temperature (400–900 °C); produces a high proportion of <strong>alkenes</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Catalytic cracking</strong>: a <strong>zeolite</strong> catalyst, slight pressure and about 450 °C; produces <strong>motor fuels and aromatic hydrocarbons</strong>. The catalyst cuts costs by allowing a lower temperature and pressure.</li>\n<li>Example: C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Combustion and pollution</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Incomplete combustion makes toxic <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> and <strong>carbon particulates</strong> (soot), which cause breathing problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Nitrogen oxides</strong> form in the high temperature of engines from N₂ and O₂ in air.</li>\n<li><strong>Sulfur dioxide</strong> from sulfur impurities causes acid rain; it is removed from power station flue gases with CaO or CaCO₃.</li>\n<li><strong>Catalytic converters</strong> (platinum/rhodium on a ceramic honeycomb) remove CO, NOₓ and unburned hydrocarbons: 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂.</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Sigma (σ) bond","A covalent bond formed by direct (head-on) overlap of orbitals between the bonding atoms."],["Hydrocarbon","A compound containing hydrogen and carbon only."]],"qa":[["Shape and bond angle around each C in an alkane","Tetrahedral, 109.5°: four σ bonds."],["Effect of chain length on alkane boiling point","Longer chain: more surface contact, stronger London forces, higher boiling point."],["Effect of branching on alkane boiling point","More branching: less surface contact, weaker London forces, lower boiling point."],["Why are alkanes unreactive?","Strong C–C and C–H σ bonds; C–H bonds are almost non-polar."],["Products of incomplete combustion of alkanes","Carbon monoxide (toxic) and/or carbon (soot), plus water."],["Conditions for alkane + halogen","Ultraviolet light: radical substitution."],["Radical substitution: initiation","Cl₂ → 2Cl• (homolytic fission by UV)."],["Radical substitution: propagation","CH₄ + Cl• → CH₃• + HCl, then CH₃• + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•."],["Radical substitution: termination","Two radicals combine, e.g. CH₃• + Cl• → CH₃Cl, or 2CH₃• → C₂H₆."],["Limitations of radical substitution","Further substitution gives a mixture of products; substitution can happen at different positions on the chain."],["Give the conditions for thermal cracking.","High temperature (400–900 °C) and high pressure (up to 7000 kPa)."],["Give the conditions for catalytic cracking.","Zeolite catalyst, slight pressure, about 450 °C."],["What does catalytic cracking mainly produce?","Motor fuels and aromatic hydrocarbons."],["How do catalytic converters remove CO and NO?","2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂ on a Pt/Rh catalyst."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Thermal cracking produces a high proportion of which type of compound?","o":["Alkanes","Alkenes","Aromatic hydrocarbons","Alcohols"],"a":1,"e":"Thermal cracking gives a high proportion of alkenes; catalytic cracking gives motor fuels and aromatics."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Alkanes","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Detailed/3.2.%20Alkanes.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Alkanes, Pollution, Cracking and Petrochemical Industry","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.2.%20Alkanes,%20Pollution,%20Cracking%20and%20Petrochemical%20Industry.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Alkanes","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Mind-Maps/3.2.%20Alkanes.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.3","mod":3,"as":true,"title":"Halogenoalkanes","short":"Nucleophilic substitution, elimination and ozone depletion.","spec":["Polarity of the C–X bond; reactivity depends on C–X bond enthalpy","Nucleophilic substitution with OH⁻, CN⁻ and NH₃: mechanisms","Elimination with ethanolic KOH to form alkenes: mechanism; OH⁻ as a base","Conditions favouring substitution or elimination","Ozone depletion: chlorine atoms from CFCs catalyse the decomposition of ozone"],"notes":"\n<h2>Halogenoalkanes</h2>\n<p>A <strong>halogenoalkane</strong> has a halogen atom in place of a hydrogen of an alkane. Like alcohols, they are classified as primary, secondary or tertiary by how many carbons are attached to the C–X carbon.</p>\n<p>The C–X bond is <strong>polar</strong>: the halogen is more electronegative, so the carbon is <strong>δ+</strong>. This δ+ carbon is attacked by <strong>nucleophiles</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>nucleophile</strong> is an <strong>electron-pair donor</strong>: a species with a lone pair that it can use to form a new covalent bond. Examples: OH⁻, H₂O, NH₃, CN⁻.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Hydrolysis: nucleophilic substitution</h2>\n<p><strong>Hydrolysis</strong> is a reaction with water or hydroxide ions that breaks a chemical bond. Heating a halogenoalkane with <strong>aqueous sodium hydroxide under reflux</strong> substitutes the halogen with –OH, forming an alcohol.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂OH + Br⁻</div>\n<svg class=\"diagram\" viewBox=\"0 0 520 150\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Nucleophilic substitution: hydroxide lone pair attacks the delta plus carbon and the C-Br bond breaks, giving the alcohol and bromide\">\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"85\">HO:⁻</text><path class=\"c1\" d=\"M42 70 Q70 45 100 70\" marker-end=\"url(#ahb)\"/>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"85\">C</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"68\" class=\"small lbl1\">δ+</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"115\" class=\"small\">H₃C</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"50\" class=\"small\">H H</text>\n<line class=\"wire\" x1=\"114\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"80\"/><text x=\"164\" y=\"85\">Br</text><text x=\"172\" y=\"68\" class=\"small lbl1\">δ−</text>\n<path class=\"c1\" d=\"M136 74 Q150 50 172 66\" marker-end=\"url(#ahb)\"/>\n<text x=\"230\" y=\"85\">→</text>\n<text x=\"280\" y=\"85\">CH₃CH₂OH  +  Br⁻</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"140\" class=\"small\">bromoethane + OH⁻ → ethanol + Br⁻ (heat under reflux with NaOH(aq))</text>\n</svg>\n<ol>\n<li>A curly arrow from a <strong>lone pair on the O of OH⁻</strong> to the δ+ carbon.</li>\n<li>A curly arrow from the <strong>C–Br bond</strong> to the Br, which leaves as Br⁻ (heterolytic fission).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The nucleophile attacks from the side <em>opposite</em> the halogen. The aqueous solvent is important: in ethanol, hot NaOH causes elimination instead.</p>\n\n<h2>Rate of hydrolysis and bond enthalpy</h2>\n<p>For primary halogenoalkanes, the rate of hydrolysis depends on the <strong>strength of the C–X bond</strong>, not on its polarity.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Bond</th><th>Bond enthalpy / kJ mol⁻¹</th><th>Rate of hydrolysis</th></tr>\n<tr><td>C–F</td><td>467</td><td>slowest (effectively none)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C–Cl</td><td>346</td><td>slow</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C–Br</td><td>290</td><td>faster</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C–I</td><td>228</td><td>fastest</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>The C–I bond is the weakest, so it breaks most easily: <strong>iodoalkanes hydrolyse fastest</strong>. Polarity would predict the opposite (C–F is the most polar), which shows that bond enthalpy is the deciding factor.</p>\n<h3>Comparing rates experimentally</h3>\n<p>Warm each halogenoalkane with <strong>aqueous silver nitrate and ethanol</strong> in a water bath at the same temperature (ethanol is a common solvent so the halogenoalkane and water mix). Water acts as the nucleophile; as each halide ion is released, it forms a silver halide precipitate:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">R–X + H₂O → R–OH + H⁺ + X⁻<br>Ag⁺(aq) + X⁻(aq) → AgX(s)</div>\n<p>Time how long the precipitate takes to appear. The <strong>iodoalkane</strong> forms a yellow precipitate first, then the bromoalkane (cream), then the chloroalkane (white), slowest. Use the same volume, the same carbon skeleton and the same number of moles to make it a fair test.</p>\n\n<h2>Organohalogen compounds and the ozone layer</h2>\n<p>The <strong>ozone layer</strong> in the stratosphere absorbs harmful <strong>UV radiation</strong>, which would otherwise cause skin cancer and cataracts. Ozone forms and breaks down naturally in a balance: O₂ + UV → 2O, then O + O₂ ⇌ O₃.</p>\n<p><strong>CFCs</strong> (chlorofluorocarbons, such as CF₂Cl₂) were used as refrigerants, aerosol propellants and solvents because they are unreactive and non-toxic. In the stratosphere, UV breaks the weakest bond, C–Cl, by homolytic fission, releasing chlorine radicals:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CF₂Cl₂ → •CF₂Cl + Cl•</div>\n<p>The chlorine radicals catalyse ozone breakdown in a chain reaction:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂<br>ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂<br>overall: O₃ + O → 2O₂</div>\n<p>Cl• is regenerated, so a single chlorine radical can destroy thousands of ozone molecules. <strong>Nitrogen monoxide radicals (•NO)</strong>, from aircraft and lightning, catalyse ozone breakdown in the same way: •NO + O₃ → •NO₂ + O₂, then •NO₂ + O → •NO + O₂.</p>\n<p>The Montreal Protocol (1987) banned CFCs. They have been replaced by HCFCs and HFCs, which break down lower in the atmosphere or contain no chlorine; C–F bonds are too strong to break in UV. The ozone layer is slowly recovering.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> \"Explain why iodoalkanes hydrolyse fastest\" needs: C–I has the lowest bond enthalpy, so it is the weakest bond and needs least energy to break. Don't mention polarity except to say it isn't the deciding factor.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Halogenoalkanes in synthesis</h2>\n<p>Halogenoalkanes are useful intermediates because the halogen can be replaced by other groups using different nucleophiles:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Nucleophile</th><th>Conditions</th><th>Product</th></tr>\n<tr><td>OH⁻</td><td>NaOH(aq), heat under reflux</td><td>alcohol</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CN⁻</td><td>NaCN in ethanol, heat</td><td>nitrile (chain + 1 carbon), 6.2.4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NH₃</td><td>excess ethanolic ammonia, heat</td><td>amine, 6.2.1</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>Comparing primary, secondary and tertiary halogenoalkanes</h2>\n<p>With aqueous silver nitrate in ethanol, tertiary halogenoalkanes hydrolyse fastest and primary slowest (for the same halogen). You only need the comparison of C–Cl, C–Br and C–I for primary halogenoalkanes, but if asked, the experiment is the same.</p>\n<h2>Writing the mechanism with water</h2>\n<p>In the silver nitrate experiment the nucleophile is <strong>water</strong>. The mechanism is the same, but water's lone pair attacks the δ+ carbon, then the intermediate loses H⁺:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH₂Br + H₂O → CH₃CH₂OH + H⁺ + Br⁻</div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> 1-chlorobutane, 1-bromobutane and 1-iodobutane are each warmed with AgNO₃ in ethanol at 50 °C. Predict the order in which precipitates appear and explain.</p>\n<p>1-iodobutane (yellow) first, then 1-bromobutane (cream), then 1-chlorobutane (white). The C–I bond has the lowest bond enthalpy, so it breaks most easily and the halide ion is released fastest.</p></div>\n<h2>Environmental chemistry: the full picture</h2>\n<p>CFCs are dangerous to ozone because they are so <strong>unreactive</strong>: they last long enough (decades) to drift up into the stratosphere. There, high-energy UV breaks the C–Cl bond. C–F bonds are stronger and are not broken, which is why HFCs (no chlorine) don't deplete ozone (though they are greenhouse gases).</p>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Define a nucleophile.</summary><div><p>An electron-pair donor.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the two propagation steps for ozone depletion by chlorine radicals.</summary><div><p>Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂; ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is the C–F bond not broken by UV in the stratosphere, while C–Cl is?</summary><div><p>The C–F bond is much stronger (higher bond enthalpy), so UV radiation there does not have enough energy to break it.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Nucleophilic substitution with cyanide and ammonia</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hydroxide</strong>: warm aqueous NaOH, under reflux → alcohol. CH₃CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂OH + Br⁻.</li>\n<li><strong>Cyanide</strong>: KCN in ethanol and water, heated under reflux → nitrile, adding one carbon to the chain. CH₃CH₂Br + CN⁻ → CH₃CH₂CN + Br⁻ (propanenitrile).</li>\n<li><strong>Ammonia</strong>: excess concentrated ammonia in ethanol, heated in a sealed tube → primary amine. CH₃CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br. The mechanism has a second step in which a second NH₃ removes H⁺ from the nitrogen. Excess ammonia reduces further substitution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In each mechanism the nucleophile’s lone pair attacks the δ+ carbon, and the C–X bond breaks heterolytically with the electrons going to the halogen. The rate depends on the <strong>C–X bond enthalpy</strong>: C–I reacts fastest because it is the weakest bond, even though C–F is the most polar.</p>\n<h2>Elimination</h2>\n<p>With <strong>KOH dissolved in ethanol</strong>, heated, OH⁻ acts as a <strong>base</strong> instead of a nucleophile. It removes an H⁺ from the carbon next to the C–X carbon, a C=C bond forms and X⁻ leaves: CH₃CHBrCH₃ + KOH → CH₂=CHCH₃ + KBr + H₂O.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong>: curly arrow from the OH⁻ lone pair to an H on the adjacent carbon; arrow from that C–H bond to form the C=C; arrow from the C–Br bond to Br.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Substitution is favoured by aqueous conditions and lower temperature, and by primary halogenoalkanes.</li>\n<li>Elimination is favoured by ethanolic conditions and higher temperature, and by tertiary halogenoalkanes. Elimination can give a mixture of alkenes, including E–Z isomers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself (AQA extras)</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>State the reagent and conditions to convert bromoethane into propanenitrile.</summary><div><p>KCN in ethanol and water, heated under reflux.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What is the role of OH⁻ in elimination?</summary><div><p>A base: it removes a proton from the carbon next to the C–Br carbon.</p></div></details>\n","terms":[["Nucleophile","An electron-pair donor."],["Hydrolysis","A reaction with water or aqueous hydroxide ions that breaks a chemical bond."],["Nucleophilic substitution","A nucleophile attacks the δ+ carbon and replaces the halogen, which leaves as a halide ion."],["Ozone layer","Ozone in the stratosphere that absorbs harmful UV radiation."]],"qa":[["Halogenoalkane + NaOH(aq): conditions and product","Heat under reflux: an alcohol (nucleophilic substitution)."],["Trend in rate of hydrolysis of halogenoalkanes","C–I fastest, C–F slowest: depends on bond enthalpy, not polarity."],["How do you compare the rates of hydrolysis of halogenoalkanes?","Add ethanol, halogenoalkane and aqueous AgNO₃ at 50 °C; time how long the precipitate takes (AgI yellow first)."],["Why is ethanol added in the hydrolysis rate experiment?","Halogenoalkanes are insoluble in water; ethanol acts as a common solvent."],["How do CFCs damage the ozone layer?","UV breaks C–Cl homolytically, releasing Cl• radicals that catalyse O₃ breakdown."],["Ozone depletion by chlorine radicals: propagation","Cl• + O₃ → ClO• + O₂, then ClO• + O → Cl• + O₂."],["Other radical that depletes ozone","NO• from aircraft and lightning."],["Curly arrows in nucleophilic substitution with OH⁻","Lone pair on OH⁻ to the δ+ carbon; C–X bond pair moves to the halogen."],["Give the reagent and conditions for elimination from a halogenoalkane.","KOH dissolved in ethanol, heated."],["Why does iodoethane react faster than chloroethane with OH⁻?","The C–I bond is weaker (lower bond enthalpy), so it breaks more easily."],["Give the conditions to make an amine from a halogenoalkane.","Excess concentrated ammonia in ethanol, heated in a sealed tube."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"In the reaction of 2-bromopropane with ethanolic KOH, what is the role of OH⁻?","o":["Nucleophile","Base","Electrophile","Catalyst"],"a":1,"e":"In elimination, OH⁻ acts as a base, removing a proton."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Halogenoalkanes","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Detailed/3.3.%20Halogenoalkanes.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Halogenoalkanes, Nucleophilic Substitution, Elimination Reactions, Uses and CFC Problems","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.3.%20Halogenoalkanes,%20Nucleophilic%20Substitution,%20Elimination%20Reactions,%20Uses%20and%20CFC%20Problems.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Halogenoalkanes","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Mind-Maps/3.3.%20Halogenoalkanes.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.4","mod":3,"as":true,"title":"Alkenes","short":"Electrophilic addition, carbocation stability and addition polymers.","spec":["Structure and bonding of alkenes; the C=C bond as a centre of high electron density","Electrophilic addition with HBr, H₂SO₄ and Br₂: mechanisms","Unsymmetrical alkenes: major and minor products from carbocation stability","Bromine water as a test for C=C","Addition polymers: repeating units, properties, and uses such as PVC with and without plasticisers"],"notes":"\n<h2>The C=C double bond</h2>\n<p>Alkenes are <strong>unsaturated hydrocarbons</strong> with at least one C=C double bond; general formula C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>. The double bond is made of one <strong>σ-bond</strong> and one <strong>π-bond</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>π-bond</strong> forms by the <strong>sideways overlap of adjacent p orbitals</strong>, above and below the plane of the bonding carbon atoms.</li>\n<li>The π-bond <strong>prevents rotation</strong> about the double bond.</li>\n<li>Each carbon of the C=C has three regions of electron density (two single bonds and the double bond), so the shape around it is <strong>trigonal planar</strong>, bond angle <strong>120°</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The π-electrons are more exposed than σ-electrons and the π-bond is weaker, so alkenes are much more reactive than alkanes. The C=C is a region of <strong>high electron density</strong> that attracts electrophiles.</p>\n\n<h2>Stereoisomerism: E/Z and cis–trans</h2>\n<p><strong>Stereoisomers</strong> have the same structural formula but a different arrangement of atoms in space. E/Z isomerism needs two things:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>a C=C double bond, so there is <strong>restricted rotation</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>two different groups</strong> attached to <strong>each</strong> carbon of the double bond.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>But-1-ene (CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃) has no E/Z isomers because one carbon carries two H atoms. But-2-ene has two:</p>\n<svg class=\"diagram\" viewBox=\"0 0 460 140\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Z-but-2-ene with both methyl groups on the same side and E-but-2-ene with methyl groups on opposite sides\">\n<g><text x=\"30\" y=\"35\">H₃C</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"35\">CH₃</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"62\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"64\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"130\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"112\" y2=\"64\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"82\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"68\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"82\" y1=\"74\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"74\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"80\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"62\" y2=\"98\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"112\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"98\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"114\">H</text><text x=\"132\" y=\"114\">H</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"136\" class=\"small lbl1\">Z (cis)-but-2-ene</text></g>\n<g transform=\"translate(240 0)\"><text x=\"30\" y=\"35\">H₃C</text><text x=\"132\" y=\"35\">H</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"62\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"64\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"130\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"112\" y2=\"64\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"82\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"68\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"82\" y1=\"74\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"74\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"80\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"62\" y2=\"98\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"112\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"98\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"114\">H</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"114\">CH₃</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"136\" class=\"small lbl1\">E (trans)-but-2-ene</text></g>\n</svg>\n<h3>The CIP priority rules</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>On each carbon of the C=C, give the attached atom with the <strong>higher atomic number</strong> the higher priority.</li>\n<li>If the attached atoms are the same, move outward along each group to the first point of difference.</li>\n<li>If the two higher-priority groups are on the <strong>same side</strong>: <strong>Z</strong> (zusammen, together). On <strong>opposite sides</strong>: <strong>E</strong> (entgegen, opposite).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Cis–trans</strong> is a special case used only when each carbon has the <em>same</em> group as one of its two (usually H): cis = same side, trans = opposite. Where it applies, cis usually corresponds to Z, but not always: in 1-bromo-1-chloropropene the CIP rule can give an answer that differs from a \"cis/trans\" reading, which is why E/Z is the general system.</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Name the isomer of CHBr=CHCl with Br and Cl on the same side.</p>\n<p>Carbon 1: Br (Z = 35) beats H. Carbon 2: Cl (17) beats H. The high-priority groups Br and Cl are on the same side: <strong>(Z)-1-bromo-2-chloroethene</strong>.</p></div>\n\n<h2>Addition reactions</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Reagent</th><th>Conditions</th><th>Product</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>H₂ (hydrogenation)</td><td>nickel catalyst, 150 °C</td><td>alkane</td><td>CH₂=CH₂ + H₂ → CH₃CH₃</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Halogen (X₂)</td><td>room temperature</td><td>dihalogenoalkane</td><td>CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hydrogen halide (HX)</td><td>room temperature (gas)</td><td>halogenoalkane</td><td>CH₂=CH₂ + HBr → CH₃CH₂Br</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Steam (H₂O(g))</td><td>H₃PO₄ catalyst, ~300 °C, 6–7 MPa</td><td>alcohol</td><td>CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O → CH₃CH₂OH</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Hydrogenation is used to harden vegetable oils into margarine. Hydration of ethene is the industrial route to ethanol (100% atom economy).</p>\n<h3>Test for unsaturation</h3>\n<p>Shake with <strong>bromine water</strong>. An alkene <strong>decolourises</strong> it (orange → colourless) because bromine adds across the C=C. Alkanes give no change.</p>\n\n<h2>Electrophilic addition: the mechanism</h2>\n<p>An <strong>electrophile</strong> is an <strong>electron-pair acceptor</strong>. The C=C's high electron density attracts it.</p>\n<svg class=\"diagram\" viewBox=\"0 0 600 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Electrophilic addition of bromine to ethene: the pi bond attacks the delta plus bromine, the Br-Br bond breaks heterolytically, then bromide attacks the carbocation\">\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"95\">H₂C</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"58\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"86\"/><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"58\" y1=\"93\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"93\"/><text x=\"94\" y=\"95\">CH₂</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"160\">Br</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"148\" class=\"small lbl1\">δ+</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"80\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"140\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"120\">Br</text><text x=\"96\" y=\"112\" class=\"small lbl1\">δ−</text>\n<path class=\"c1\" d=\"M74 82 Q40 120 72 148\" marker-end=\"url(#ahb)\"/><path class=\"c1\" d=\"M86 133 Q115 128 108 116\" marker-end=\"url(#ahb)\"/>\n<text x=\"140\" y=\"95\">→</text>\n<text x=\"180\" y=\"95\">H₂C</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"216\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"90\"/><text x=\"244\" y=\"95\">CH₂</text><text x=\"276\" y=\"84\" class=\"small\">+</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"196\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"122\"/><text x=\"186\" y=\"138\">Br</text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"140\">:Br⁻</text><path class=\"c1\" d=\"M306 126 Q300 100 278 96\" marker-end=\"url(#ahb)\"/>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"95\">→</text>\n<text x=\"390\" y=\"95\">H₂C</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"426\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"90\"/><text x=\"454\" y=\"95\">CH₂</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"406\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"122\"/><text x=\"396\" y=\"138\">Br</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"470\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"470\" y2=\"122\"/><text x=\"460\" y=\"138\">Br</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"182\" class=\"small\">alkene + Br₂ → carbocation intermediate + Br⁻ → 1,2-dibromoethane</text>\n</svg>\n<ol>\n<li>As Br₂ approaches the C=C, the π-electrons repel the electrons in Br–Br, inducing a dipole: the nearer Br becomes δ+.</li>\n<li>A curly arrow goes from the <strong>π-bond</strong> to the δ+ Br. A second arrow goes from the <strong>Br–Br bond</strong> to the δ− Br, which leaves as Br⁻ (heterolytic fission).</li>\n<li>This forms a <strong>carbocation</strong> intermediate (a carbon with a positive charge, only three bonds).</li>\n<li>A curly arrow from a <strong>lone pair</strong> on Br⁻ to the C⁺ forms the second C–Br bond.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With HBr the mechanism is the same, but the H–Br bond is already polar (H<sup>δ+</sup>): the arrow goes from the π-bond to H, and from the H–Br bond to Br.</p>\n<h3>Markownikoff's rule</h3>\n<p>When HBr adds to an <strong>unsymmetrical alkene</strong> like propene, two products are possible: 1-bromopropane and 2-bromopropane. The major product is the one formed via the <strong>more stable carbocation</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carbocation stability: <strong>tertiary &gt; secondary &gt; primary</strong>. Alkyl groups push electron density toward the positive carbon (they are electron-donating), spreading out the charge. More alkyl groups, more stable.</li>\n<li>Propene + HBr: H adds to the end CH₂, giving the secondary carbocation CH₃C⁺HCH₃, which gives <strong>2-bromopropane</strong> as the major product.</li>\n<li>Markownikoff's rule in words: the H atom adds to the carbon of the C=C that already has more H atoms.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Addition polymers</h2>\n<p>Alkenes join together in <strong>addition polymerisation</strong>: the double bonds open and monomers link into a long saturated chain. No other product forms.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">n CH₂=CHCl → –[CH₂–CHCl]ₙ– &nbsp;&nbsp; (chloroethene → poly(chloroethene), PVC)</div>\n<ul>\n<li>To draw the <strong>repeat unit</strong>, take the two carbons of the C=C, make the double bond single, put the side groups on, and draw extension bonds through square brackets.</li>\n<li>To find the <strong>monomer</strong> from a repeat unit, take the two main-chain carbons and put a double bond between them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Dealing with waste polymers</h3>\n<p>Addition polymers are unreactive and <strong>non-biodegradable</strong>, so they persist in landfill and the environment.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Recycling</strong>: sorting, melting and remoulding. Conserves finite crude oil and reduces landfill, but sorting by type is expensive.</li>\n<li><strong>PVC</strong> recycling is hazardous because of its chlorine content: burning releases HCl and toxic chlorinated compounds. Solvents can dissolve and recover it.</li>\n<li><strong>Energy from waste</strong>: burning polymers to generate electricity.</li>\n<li><strong>Feedstock recycling</strong>: chemical or thermal processes that break polymers down into monomers or other useful chemicals for new materials.</li>\n<li>Toxic gases such as HCl from burning are removed by <strong>neutralising</strong> them with a base in the chimney.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Biodegradable</strong> polymers (made from starch, cellulose, or polymers like poly(lactic acid)) are broken down by microorganisms. <strong>Photodegradable</strong> polymers contain bonds that break when they absorb light. Both reduce pollution, but need suitable conditions to break down.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the electrophilic addition mechanism needs four things: the dipole on Br–Br (or H–Br), the arrow from the C=C to the δ+ atom, the arrow from the bond to the δ− atom, and the lone pair on Br⁻ attacking the carbocation (with the + charge shown on carbon).</p></blockquote>\n\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Explain why the major product of propene + HBr is 2-bromopropane.</p>\n<p>The H can add to either carbon of the C=C. Adding to the end carbon gives a <strong>secondary</strong> carbocation, CH₃C⁺HCH₃; adding to the middle carbon gives a <strong>primary</strong> carbocation, CH₃CH₂CH₂⁺. The secondary carbocation is more stable (two electron-donating alkyl groups), so it forms more readily and Br⁻ adds to it, giving 2-bromopropane as the major product.</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Does pent-2-ene show E/Z isomerism? Explain.</summary><div><p>Yes: each carbon of the C=C has two different groups (C1: H and CH₃; C2: H and C₂H₅), and rotation about C=C is restricted.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Draw the repeat unit of poly(propene).</summary><div><p>–[CH₂–CH(CH₃)]– with extension bonds.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What are the conditions for hydrogenating an alkene?</summary><div><p>H₂ with a nickel catalyst at about 150 °C.</p></div></details>\n<h2>AQA alkene details</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Concentrated sulfuric acid</strong> adds across C=C by electrophilic addition to give an alkyl hydrogensulfate; adding water then hydrolyses it to an alcohol (with H₂SO₄ regenerated as a catalyst).</li>\n<li>The major product comes from the more stable carbocation: tertiary > secondary > primary, because alkyl groups push electrons towards the positive carbon (positive inductive effect).</li>\n<li><strong>Addition polymers</strong> such as poly(chloroethene) (PVC) are unreactive because the chains are saturated. Adding a <strong>plasticiser</strong> pushes the chains apart so they slide over each other, making PVC flexible (for cable insulation) instead of rigid (for window frames).</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Stereoisomers","Compounds with the same structural formula but a different arrangement of atoms in space."],["Electrophile","An electron-pair acceptor."],["Pi (π) bond","Sideways overlap of adjacent p orbitals above and below the bonding atoms."],["E/Z isomerism","Stereoisomerism from restricted rotation about a C=C bond, with two different groups on each carbon of the double bond."],["Cis–trans isomerism","A special case of E/Z isomerism where two of the substituent groups on the C=C are the same."],["Electrophilic addition","An electrophile attacks the electron-rich C=C, and the pi bond breaks to form two new σ bonds."],["Markownikoff’s rule","H adds to the carbon that already has more hydrogens, giving the more stable carbocation (major product)."],["Addition polymer","A long-chain molecule formed when many alkene monomers join together."]],"qa":[["Shape and bond angle around each C of C=C","Trigonal planar, 120°."],["Why can’t a C=C bond rotate?","The pi bond would have to break: the p orbitals must stay overlapping."],["Why are alkenes more reactive than alkanes?","The pi bond has a lower bond enthalpy than a σ bond and is a region of high electron density."],["How are E and Z assigned?","CIP rules: the higher atomic number group on each carbon. Same side = Z, opposite sides = E."],["Alkene + H₂: conditions","Nickel catalyst, about 150 °C: forms an alkane (hydrogenation)."],["Alkene + steam: conditions","Phosphoric acid (H₃PO₄) catalyst, about 300 °C, 60–70 atm: forms an alcohol."],["Test for a C=C bond","Bromine water turns from orange to colourless."],["Order of carbocation stability","Tertiary > secondary > primary (more alkyl groups push electrons towards the positive carbon)."],["How is waste polymer dealt with?","Recycling, combustion for energy, feedstock for cracking; PVC is dehydrochlorinated before recycling."],["What are biodegradable and photodegradable polymers?","Biodegradable: broken down by microorganisms (e.g. from starch). Photodegradable: break down in light."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Alkenes","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Detailed/3.4.%20Alkenes.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Alkenes - Isomerism, Electrophilic Addition, Polymerisation and Polymers","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.4.%20Alkenes%20-%20Isomerism,%20Electrophilic%20Addition,%20Polymerisation%20and%20Polymers.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Alkenes","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Mind-Maps/3.4.%20Alkenes.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.5","mod":3,"as":true,"title":"Alcohols","short":"Making ethanol, classifying and oxidising alcohols, and elimination.","spec":["Alcohol production: hydration of alkenes (steam, H₃PO₄ catalyst) and fermentation of glucose; bioethanol and carbon neutrality","Primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols","Oxidation with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇: aldehydes (distil), carboxylic acids (reflux) and ketones; tertiary alcohols resist","Tests for aldehydes with Fehling’s solution and Tollens’ reagent","Elimination of water from alcohols to form alkenes (acid-catalysed dehydration): mechanism","Required practical 5: distillation of a product from a reaction"],"notes":"\n<h2>Properties of alcohols</h2>\n<p>Alcohols contain the <strong>hydroxyl</strong> group, –OH; general formula C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+1</sub>OH. The O–H bond is polar, so alcohols can form <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low volatility / higher boiling points</strong> than alkanes of similar M<sub>r</sub>: extra energy is needed to break hydrogen bonds between alcohol molecules. Ethanol (M<sub>r</sub> 46) boils at 78 °C; propane (M<sub>r</sub> 44) at −42 °C.</li>\n<li><strong>Soluble in water</strong>: alcohol molecules form hydrogen bonds with water. Solubility falls as the chain gets longer, because the non-polar hydrocarbon part becomes more important.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classifying alcohols</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary (1°)</strong>: the C bonded to –OH is attached to <strong>one</strong> other carbon (or none): ethanol, propan-1-ol.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary (2°)</strong>: the C–OH carbon is attached to <strong>two</strong> carbons: propan-2-ol.</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary (3°)</strong>: the C–OH carbon is attached to <strong>three</strong> carbons: 2-methylpropan-2-ol.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Combustion</h2>\n<div class=\"rxn\">C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O</div>\n<p>Ethanol made by fermenting sugars is used as a biofuel.</p>\n\n<h2>Oxidation</h2>\n<p>The oxidising agent is <strong>acidified potassium dichromate(VI)</strong>, K₂Cr₂O₇ / H₂SO₄. In equations, oxygen from the oxidising agent is written as <strong>[O]</strong>. When the alcohol is oxidised, the orange Cr₂O₇²⁻ is reduced to green Cr³⁺: <strong>orange → green</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Primary alcohols</h3>\n<p>A primary alcohol is oxidised first to an <strong>aldehyde</strong>, then further to a <strong>carboxylic acid</strong>. Which you get depends on the apparatus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aldehyde</strong>: gently heat with dichromate and <strong>distil</strong> the aldehyde off as soon as it forms (aldehydes have lower boiling points than alcohols, because they can't hydrogen bond to each other). This removes it before it is oxidised further.<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH₂OH + [O] → CH₃CHO + H₂O</div></li>\n<li><strong>Carboxylic acid</strong>: use <strong>excess</strong> oxidising agent and <strong>heat under reflux</strong>, so any aldehyde formed stays in the flask and is oxidised fully.<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH₂OH + 2[O] → CH₃COOH + H₂O</div></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Secondary alcohols</h3>\n<p>Oxidised to a <strong>ketone</strong>, under reflux. Ketones can't be oxidised further.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH(OH)CH₃ + [O] → CH₃COCH₃ + H₂O</div>\n<h3>Tertiary alcohols</h3>\n<p><strong>Not oxidised</strong>: the carbon with the –OH has no hydrogen atom to lose. The dichromate stays <strong>orange</strong>. This is a way of telling tertiary alcohols apart.</p>\n<h3>Reflux and distillation</h3>\n<p><strong>Reflux</strong> means heating with a vertical condenser fitted to the flask: vapours condense and run back into the flask, so the mixture can be heated for a long time without losing volatile reactants or products. Use anti-bumping granules, and never seal the top. <strong>Distillation</strong> uses a sloping condenser to collect liquid that boils off at a particular temperature.</p>\n\n<h2>Elimination: dehydration to alkenes</h2>\n<p>Heating an alcohol with an <strong>acid catalyst</strong> (concentrated H₃PO₄ or concentrated H₂SO₄) removes a water molecule, forming an alkene.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH₂OH → CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O</div>\n<p>Unsymmetrical alcohols can give more than one alkene: butan-2-ol gives but-1-ene and both E- and Z-but-2-ene.</p>\n\n<h2>Substitution with halide ions</h2>\n<p>Alcohols react with halide ions in the presence of an acid to form halogenoalkanes. To make a bromoalkane, heat the alcohol with <strong>sodium bromide and concentrated sulfuric acid</strong>, which react to make HBr in the flask:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">NaBr + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HBr<br>CH₃CH₂OH + HBr → CH₃CH₂Br + H₂O</div>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> a classic question: \"how would you make ethanal rather than ethanoic acid from ethanol?\" Answer: distil the product off as it forms, and don't use excess dichromate. For the acid: excess dichromate, heat under reflux.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Equations for oxidation: writing them with [O]</h2>\n<p>Count how many oxygen atoms are added (and hydrogens removed as water):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary alcohol → aldehyde: RCH₂OH + [O] → RCHO + H₂O</li>\n<li>Primary alcohol → acid: RCH₂OH + 2[O] → RCOOH + H₂O</li>\n<li>Aldehyde → acid: RCHO + [O] → RCOOH</li>\n<li>Secondary alcohol → ketone: R₂CHOH + [O] → R₂CO + H₂O</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Name the products of oxidising butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol with acidified dichromate.</p>\n<p>Butan-1-ol (primary): <strong>butanal</strong> by distillation, or <strong>butanoic acid</strong> under reflux. Butan-2-ol (secondary): <strong>butanone</strong>. 2-methylpropan-2-ol (tertiary): <strong>no reaction</strong>, stays orange.</p></div>\n<h2>Why aldehydes can be distilled off</h2>\n<p>An aldehyde has a C=O but no O–H, so its molecules can't form hydrogen bonds with each other (only weaker permanent dipole–dipole forces). It has a lower boiling point than the alcohol it was made from (ethanal 21 °C, ethanol 78 °C), so it evaporates out of the warm mixture and can be collected before further oxidation.</p>\n<h2>Dehydration: predicting all the products</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Butan-2-ol is heated with concentrated phosphoric acid. Name all the alkene products.</p>\n<p>H can be removed from carbon 1 or carbon 3 (either side of the C–OH). From C1: <strong>but-1-ene</strong>. From C3: <strong>but-2-ene</strong>, which has <strong>E and Z</strong> isomers. Three products in total.</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does ethanol dissolve in water but hexan-1-ol does not dissolve well?</summary><div><p>Both can hydrogen bond with water through –OH, but hexan-1-ol has a long non-polar hydrocarbon chain that cannot, so its solubility is much lower.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What colour change shows a positive result with acidified potassium dichromate?</summary><div><p>Orange to green.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Classify propan-2-ol and name its oxidation product.</summary><div><p>Secondary alcohol; it is oxidised to propanone.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Making ethanol and dehydrating alcohols</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hydration of ethene</strong>: steam, a phosphoric acid catalyst, about 300 °C and 6–7 MPa. Fast, continuous and gives pure ethanol, but uses a finite resource.</li>\n<li><strong>Fermentation</strong>: C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂, using yeast at 30–40 °C, with no air (anaerobic). Uses a renewable resource but is slow and batch, and gives impure, dilute ethanol that must be distilled.</li>\n<li><strong>Bioethanol</strong> is described as carbon neutral, because CO₂ absorbed by photosynthesis (6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂) balances the CO₂ released by fermentation and combustion. In practice, farming, transport and distillation release extra CO₂.</li>\n<li><strong>Dehydration</strong>: heat an alcohol with concentrated H₂SO₄ or H₃PO₄ to eliminate water and form an alkene. The mechanism: the OH is protonated, water leaves to give a carbocation, and H⁺ is lost from an adjacent carbon. This gives a route to polymers that does not rely on crude oil.</li>\n<li><strong>Oxidation</strong>: AQA uses acidified potassium dichromate(VI), shown as [O] in equations. Primary → aldehyde (distil off as it forms) → carboxylic acid (heat under reflux with excess oxidising agent). Secondary → ketone. Tertiary: no reaction (no H on the C–OH carbon).</li>\n<li><strong>Fehling’s solution</strong>: warm with an aldehyde, and the blue solution forms a <strong>red precipitate</strong> of Cu₂O. Ketones give no change.</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Primary alcohol","The –OH carbon is attached to one other carbon atom (or none)."],["Secondary alcohol","The –OH carbon is attached to two other carbon atoms."],["Tertiary alcohol","The –OH carbon is attached to three other carbon atoms."]],"qa":[["Why are alcohols less volatile and more water-soluble than alkanes?","They form hydrogen bonds (with each other and with water)."],["Oxidising agent for alcohols","Acidified potassium dichromate(VI): K₂Cr₂O₇ / H₂SO₄."],["Colour change when dichromate oxidises an alcohol","Orange to green (Cr₂O₇²⁻ → Cr³⁺)."],["Primary alcohol → aldehyde: conditions","Gentle heat with acidified dichromate; distil off the aldehyde as it forms."],["Primary alcohol → carboxylic acid: conditions","Excess acidified dichromate, heat under reflux."],["Oxidation product of a secondary alcohol","A ketone (heat under reflux with acidified dichromate)."],["What happens when a tertiary alcohol is heated with acidified dichromate?","No reaction: it stays orange."],["Dehydration of an alcohol","Heat with an acid catalyst (concentrated H₂SO₄ or H₃PO₄): elimination forms an alkene + water."],["Alcohol → halogenoalkane","Heat with NaBr and H₂SO₄ (makes HBr): substitution."],["Give the conditions for fermentation.","Yeast, 30–40 °C, anaerobic (no air)."],["Why is bioethanol described as carbon neutral?","CO₂ absorbed by photosynthesis balances the CO₂ released by fermentation and combustion."],["What do you see when an aldehyde is warmed with Fehling’s solution?","The blue solution forms a brick-red precipitate."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which alcohol is not oxidised by acidified potassium dichromate(VI)?","o":["Propan-1-ol","Propan-2-ol","2-methylpropan-2-ol","Butan-2-ol"],"a":2,"e":"2-methylpropan-2-ol is tertiary: there is no H on the carbon bonded to OH."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":["rp5"],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Alcohols","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Detailed/3.5.%20Alcohols.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Alcohols, Oxidation, IR Spec, Biofuels and Industrial Preparation of Ethanol","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.5.%20Alcohols,%20Oxidation,%20IR%20Spec,%20Biofuels%20and%20Industrial%20Preparation%20of%20Ethanol.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Alcohols","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Mind-Maps/3.5.%20Alcohols.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"},{"set":"Required practical guides","label":"RP 5: Distillation of a reaction product","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Practical-Skills/RP%2005%20-%20Distillation%20of%20a%20reaction%20product.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.6","mod":3,"as":true,"title":"Organic analysis","short":"Test-tube tests, high-resolution mass spectrometry and infrared spectroscopy.","spec":["Identifying functional groups by test-tube reactions: alkenes, alcohols, aldehydes, carboxylic acids (required practical 6)","High-resolution mass spectrometry: precise Mr to find molecular formulae","Infrared spectroscopy: bond absorptions, the fingerprint region and identifying functional groups","Infrared absorption by CO₂, methane and water vapour and the greenhouse effect"],"notes":"\n<h2>Infrared spectroscopy</h2>\n<p>Covalent bonds vibrate (stretch and bend). Each bond absorbs infrared radiation at particular frequencies that make it vibrate more. An IR spectrum shows how much radiation is absorbed (as % transmittance) at each <strong>wavenumber</strong> (cm⁻¹). Absorptions show up as <strong>dips</strong>, pointing downward.</p>\n<h3>IR and the atmosphere</h3>\n<p>Molecules such as CO₂, H₂O and CH₄ absorb IR radiation emitted from the Earth's surface, then re-emit it in all directions, keeping heat in the lower atmosphere: the <strong>greenhouse effect</strong>. Increased levels of these gases from human activity are linked to <strong>global warming</strong>. Scientists monitor greenhouse gases using IR spectroscopy.</p>\n<h3>Breathalysers</h3>\n<p>Modern breathalysers measure the absorption of the C–H bonds (and the O–H/C–O bonds) of ethanol in breath. The intensity of absorption shows the ethanol concentration.</p>\n<h3>Reading an IR spectrum</h3>\n<p>The AQA Data Booklet lists the absorption ranges. The three to know well:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Bond</th><th>Compound</th><th>Wavenumber / cm⁻¹</th><th>Appearance</th></tr>\n<tr><td>O–H</td><td>alcohol</td><td>3200–3600</td><td>broad</td></tr>\n<tr><td>O–H</td><td>carboxylic acid</td><td>2500–3300</td><td>very broad</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C=O</td><td>aldehyde, ketone, acid, ester</td><td>1630–1820</td><td>strong, sharp</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C–O</td><td>alcohol, ester, acid</td><td>1000–1300</td><td>strong</td></tr>\n<tr><td>N–H</td><td>amine, amide</td><td>3300–3500</td><td>medium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C–H</td><td>most organics</td><td>2850–3100</td><td>present almost always</td></tr></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alcohol</strong>: broad O–H (3200–3600) and C–O, no C=O.</li>\n<li><strong>Aldehyde or ketone</strong>: C=O (about 1700), no O–H.</li>\n<li><strong>Carboxylic acid</strong>: very broad O–H (2500–3300) <em>and</em> C=O.</li>\n<li><strong>Ester</strong>: C=O and C–O, no O–H.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The region below about 1500 cm⁻¹ (the <strong>fingerprint region</strong>) is unique to each compound and can be matched against a database.</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Ethanol is oxidised. The product's spectrum shows a strong absorption at 1720 cm⁻¹ and a very broad one at 2500–3300 cm⁻¹. What is it?</p>\n<p>C=O and the very broad carboxylic acid O–H: the product is <strong>ethanoic acid</strong>, so the alcohol was fully oxidised.</p></div>\n\n<h2>Mass spectrometry of organic compounds</h2>\n<p>In the mass spectrometer, an organic molecule loses an electron to form a <strong>molecular ion</strong>, M⁺. The peak with the <strong>highest m/z</strong> (ignoring the small M+1 peak caused by ¹³C) is the molecular ion peak, and its m/z is the <strong>relative molecular mass</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Fragmentation</h3>\n<p>Some molecular ions break into a positive <strong>fragment ion</strong> and a radical. Only the ion is detected.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">[CH₃CH₂CH₃]⁺ → CH₃CH₂⁺ + •CH₃ &nbsp;&nbsp; (peak at m/z 29)</div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>m/z</th><th>Likely fragment</th></tr>\n<tr><td>15</td><td>CH₃⁺</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>C₂H₅⁺ or CHO⁺</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>CH₂OH⁺ (primary alcohol)</td></tr><tr><td>43</td><td>C₃H₇⁺ or CH₃CO⁺</td></tr><tr><td>45</td><td>COOH⁺</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Fragments help tell isomers apart: propan-1-ol gives a peak at 31 (CH₂OH⁺); propan-2-ol gives a peak at 45 (CH₃CHOH⁺) instead.</p>\n\n<h2>Putting it together</h2>\n<p>Combine evidence to identify a compound:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Elemental analysis</strong> (percentage composition) gives the empirical formula (2.1.3).</li>\n<li>The <strong>molecular ion peak</strong> gives M<sub>r</sub>, so the molecular formula.</li>\n<li><strong>IR</strong> identifies the functional groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Fragment peaks</strong> help decide the structure.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A compound is 66.7% C, 11.1% H, 22.2% O. Its molecular ion is at m/z 72. The IR shows a strong peak at 1715 cm⁻¹ and no broad O–H. The mass spectrum has a peak at m/z 43. Identify it.</p>\n$$\\mathrm{C}:\\ \\dfrac{66.7}{12.0}=5.56$$$$\\mathrm{H}:\\ \\dfrac{11.1}{1.0}=11.1$$$$\\mathrm{O}:\\ \\dfrac{22.2}{16.0}=1.39$$<p>Dividing by 1.39 gives 4 : 8 : 1, so the empirical formula is C₄H₈O (M = 72), which is also the molecular formula.</p>\n<p>C=O, no O–H: aldehyde or ketone. m/z 43 = CH₃CO⁺. The compound is <strong>butanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃</strong>.</p></div>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> when you use a spectrum, quote the wavenumber (or m/z) <em>and</em> the bond or fragment it shows. \"Peak at 1720 cm⁻¹ shows C=O\" earns the mark; \"there is a C=O\" does not.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Interpreting an IR spectrum step by step</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Look at 2500–3600 cm⁻¹: a broad O–H? Very broad (acid) or broad (alcohol)? A sharper N–H?</li>\n<li>Look at about 1700 cm⁻¹: a strong C=O?</li>\n<li>Look at 1000–1300 cm⁻¹: a C–O?</li>\n<li>Put it together and check against the formula.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A compound C₃H₆O₂ has IR peaks at 1740 and 1200 cm⁻¹ and nothing broad above 2500 except C–H. Which functional group?</p>\n<p>C=O (1740) and C–O (1200), no O–H: an <strong>ester</strong> (methyl ethanoate or ethyl methanoate).</p></div>\n<h2>Using IR to follow a reaction</h2>\n<p>IR is used to check how far a reaction has gone. Oxidising propan-1-ol: the starting alcohol shows broad O–H only; propanal shows C=O only; propanoic acid shows very broad O–H <em>and</em> C=O. If a product sample shows both broad alcohol O–H and C=O, some unreacted alcohol remains.</p>\n<h2>Mass spectrometry: isotope peaks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M+1 peak</strong>: a small peak one unit above M⁺, caused by molecules containing one <sup>13</sup>C atom (1.1% of carbon). Ignore it when finding M<sub>r</sub>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chlorine</strong>: M⁺ and M+2 peaks in a 3 : 1 ratio (<sup>35</sup>Cl and <sup>37</sup>Cl).</li>\n<li><strong>Bromine</strong>: M⁺ and M+2 peaks of about equal height (<sup>79</sup>Br and <sup>81</sup>Br).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A halogenoalkane gives peaks at m/z 64 and 66 in a 3 : 1 ratio, and a fragment at 29. Identify it.</p>\n<p>3 : 1 M⁺ and M+2: one Cl atom. M<sub>r</sub> = 64 → C₂H₅Cl (24 + 5 + 35 = 64). Fragment 29 = C₂H₅⁺. <strong>Chloroethane</strong>.</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does every organic compound show absorption near 2900 cm⁻¹?</summary><div><p>Almost all organic compounds contain C–H bonds, which absorb between 2850 and 3100 cm⁻¹.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How would you use IR to show that ethanol has been oxidised to ethanal, not ethanoic acid?</summary><div><p>Ethanal: C=O absorption (about 1720–1740) with no broad O–H. Ethanoic acid would also show a very broad O–H at 2500–3300.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What species gives the molecular ion peak?</summary><div><p>The molecule that has lost one electron, M⁺; its m/z equals the relative molecular mass.</p></div></details>\n<h2>High-resolution mass spectrometry</h2>\n<p>High-resolution mass spectrometers measure m/z to four or more decimal places. Because atomic masses are not exact whole numbers (¹H = 1.0078, ¹²C = 12.0000, ¹⁴N = 14.0031, ¹⁶O = 15.9949), compounds with the same integer M<sub>r</sub> can be told apart.</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A molecular ion has m/z = 44.0262. Is the compound C₃H₈, CO₂ or C₂H₄O?</p>\n<p>C₃H₈ = 44.0624; CO₂ = 43.9898; C₂H₄O = 2(12.0000) + 4(1.0078) + 15.9949 = <strong>44.0261</strong>. The compound is <strong>C₂H₄O</strong>.</p></div>\n<h2>Infrared and the greenhouse effect</h2>\n<p>Bonds absorb infrared radiation at characteristic wavenumbers (given in Table A of the AQA Data Booklet). CO₂, methane and water vapour absorb infrared radiation emitted from the Earth’s surface, which keeps the lower atmosphere warm: the greenhouse effect. Their rising concentrations are linked to global warming.</p>\n","terms":[["Fragment ion","A positive ion formed when the molecular ion breaks up in a mass spectrometer."],["Molecular ion (M⁺)","The positive ion formed by loss of an electron from the whole molecule; its m/z gives Mr."],["High-resolution mass spectrometry","Measures m/z to several decimal places so compounds with the same integer Mr can be distinguished."]],"qa":[["IR absorption of O–H in alcohols","Broad peak at 3200–3600 cm⁻¹."],["IR absorption of O–H in carboxylic acids","Very broad peak at 2500–3300 cm⁻¹."],["IR absorption of C=O","Sharp, strong peak at 1630–1820 cm⁻¹."],["IR absorption of C–O","1000–1300 cm⁻¹."],["Which bonds absorb IR, and why does it matter?","Bonds vibrate (stretch, bend) by absorbing IR. CO₂, H₂O and CH₄ do this in the atmosphere: greenhouse effect."],["Uses of IR spectroscopy","Identifying functional groups; breathalysers (ethanol); monitoring pollutants such as CO and NO."],["Mass spectrum: what does the M+1 peak come from?","Molecules containing one ¹³C atom."],["Common fragment at m/z = 29","C₂H₅⁺ (or CHO⁺)."],["Common fragment at m/z = 15 and 43","CH₃⁺ and C₃H₇⁺ (or CH₃CO⁺)."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"A compound gives a molecular ion at m/z = 28.0313. Which is it? (C = 12.0000, H = 1.0078, N = 14.0031, O = 15.9949)","o":["CO","N₂","C₂H₄","CH₂N"],"a":2,"e":"C₂H₄ = 24.0000 + 4.0312 = 28.0312."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":["rp6"],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 1 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-I/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%201%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Organic Analysis","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Detailed/3.6.%20Organic%20Analysis.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: IR Spectroscopy and Global Warming","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.6.%20IR%20Spectroscopy%20and%20Global%20Warming.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Identifying Ions - Isotopes and Mass Spec","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.6.%20Identifying%20Ions%20-%20Isotopes%20and%20Mass%20Spec.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Mass Spectrometry","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Knockhardy/3.6.%20Mass%20Spectrometry.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Organic Analysis","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Mind-Maps/3.6.%20Organic%20Analysis.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 1","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-I/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20I%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"},{"set":"Required practical guides","label":"RP 6: Organic tests","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Practical-Skills/RP%2006%20-%20Organic%20tests.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.7","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Optical isomerism","short":"Chiral centres, enantiomers, racemates and plane-polarised light.","spec":["Optical isomerism from an asymmetric (chiral) carbon atom with four different groups","Enantiomers rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions","A racemic mixture (racemate) is optically inactive","Explaining why nucleophilic addition to a planar carbonyl gives a racemate"],"notes":"<h2>Optical isomerism (chirality)</h2>\n<p>A <strong>chiral centre</strong> is a carbon atom attached to <strong>four different atoms or groups</strong>. Mark it with an asterisk *. A molecule with one chiral centre exists as two <strong>optical isomers</strong> (enantiomers): stereoisomers that are <strong>non-superimposable mirror images</strong> of each other, like left and right hands.</p>\n<h3>Drawing optical isomers</h3>\n<p>Draw the chiral carbon with two bonds in the plane of the paper (plain lines), one coming <strong>towards</strong> you (wedge) and one going <strong>away</strong> (dashed wedge), arranged tetrahedrally. Then draw the mirror image with a mirror line between them.</p>\n<svg class=\"diagram\" viewBox=\"0 0 460 170\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two optical isomers of alanine drawn as 3D mirror images either side of a dashed mirror line\">\n<g><text x=\"90\" y=\"85\">C</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"98\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"98\" y2=\"44\"/><text x=\"78\" y=\"38\">COOH</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"90\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"56\" y2=\"110\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"124\">H₃C</text>\n<path class=\"fill1\" d=\"M104 88 L140 110 L134 116 Z\"/><text x=\"138\" y=\"130\">NH₂</text><line class=\"wire dash\" x1=\"108\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"64\"/><text x=\"144\" y=\"64\">H</text></g>\n<line class=\"dimline dash\" x1=\"230\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"150\"/><text x=\"212\" y=\"166\" class=\"small\">mirror</text>\n<g><text x=\"354\" y=\"85\">C</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"362\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"362\" y2=\"44\"/><text x=\"342\" y=\"38\">COOH</text><line class=\"wire\" x1=\"370\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"404\" y2=\"110\"/><text x=\"406\" y=\"124\">CH₃</text>\n<path class=\"fill1\" d=\"M356 88 L320 110 L326 116 Z\"/><text x=\"292\" y=\"130\">H₂N</text><line class=\"wire dash\" x1=\"352\" y1=\"78\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"64\"/><text x=\"306\" y=\"64\">H</text></g>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li>All α-amino acids except glycine (where R = H, so the carbon has two H atoms) are chiral.</li>\n<li>Optical isomers have identical chemical reactions (except with other chiral molecules) and physical properties, but rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions. A 50 : 50 mixture is a <strong>racemic mixture</strong> and doesn't rotate the light.</li>\n<li>In drugs, often only one enantiomer is active; the other may be inactive or harmful. Synthesising a single enantiomer (using chiral catalysts, enzymes or natural chiral starting materials) avoids this, though it can be costly.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Does butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, have optical isomers?</p>\n<p>Carbon 2 is bonded to H, OH, CH₃ and C₂H₅: four different groups, so it is a chiral centre. <strong>Yes</strong>: two optical isomers.</p>\n<p>Butan-1-ol has no carbon with four different groups, so no optical isomers.</p></div>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> when you draw optical isomers, make sure the four groups are the same in both and that the second really is the mirror image: the easiest way is to swap the positions of any two groups.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Spotting chiral centres in bigger molecules</h2>\n<p>Check each carbon in turn. Ignore CH₃, CH₂ and any C in a C=C or C=O (they can't have four different groups). For each remaining carbon, list its four groups, looking all the way along each chain if two groups start the same.</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> How many chiral centres are in 2-hydroxypropanoic acid (lactic acid), CH₃CH(OH)COOH?</p>\n<p>Carbon 2 has H, OH, CH₃ and COOH: four different groups. <strong>One chiral centre</strong>, two optical isomers.</p></div>\n<h2>Racemic mixtures in synthesis</h2>\n<p>When a nucleophile attacks a planar group (such as C=O in nucleophilic addition) it can approach equally from either side, making a 50 : 50 mixture of the two optical isomers: a <strong>racemic mixture</strong>, which is optically inactive. Pharmaceutical synthesis often needs extra steps to separate them, or chiral catalysts to make only one.</p>\n","terms":[["Chiral centre","A carbon atom attached to four different atoms or groups of atoms."],["Optical isomers","Stereoisomers that are non-superimposable mirror images of each other."],["Optical isomers (enantiomers)","Non-superimposable mirror images of a chiral molecule."]],"qa":[["How do you identify a chiral centre?","A carbon with four different groups attached."],["Drawing optical isomers","Two 3D tetrahedral structures that are mirror images (wedge and dash bonds)."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Why is a racemic mixture optically inactive?","o":["It has no chiral centre","The two enantiomers rotate plane-polarised light equally in opposite directions","It absorbs polarised light","It is a mixture of structural isomers"],"a":1,"e":"Equal amounts of each enantiomer cancel each other’s rotation."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Optical Isomers","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.07.%20Optical%20Isomers.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Isomerism - Structural, E_Z, Optical and Stereoisomers","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.07.%20Isomerism%20-%20Structural,%20E_Z,%20Optical%20and%20Stereoisomers.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Optical Isomerism","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.07.%20Optical%20Isomerism.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.8","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Aldehydes and ketones","short":"Oxidation, reduction with NaBH₄, and nucleophilic addition of KCN.","spec":["Aldehydes are oxidised to carboxylic acids; ketones are not","Tollens’ reagent and Fehling’s solution to tell aldehydes from ketones","Reduction to alcohols with NaBH₄: nucleophilic addition mechanism","Nucleophilic addition of KCN followed by dilute acid to form hydroxynitriles: mechanism and racemic products; the hazard of KCN"],"notes":"\n<h2>Aldehydes and ketones</h2>\n<p>Both contain the <strong>carbonyl group</strong>, C=O.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aldehydes</strong>: the C=O is at the <strong>end</strong> of the chain, –CHO. Named -al: methanal HCHO, ethanal CH₃CHO, propanal.</li>\n<li><strong>Ketones</strong>: the C=O is <strong>within</strong> the chain, bonded to two carbons. Named -one: propanone CH₃COCH₃, butanone, pentan-2-one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The C=O bond is polar (O is δ−, C is δ+). Unlike C=C, it is attacked by <strong>nucleophiles</strong> at the δ+ carbon.</p>\n\n<h2>Oxidation of aldehydes</h2>\n<p>Aldehydes are oxidised to <strong>carboxylic acids</strong> by heating under reflux with acidified dichromate (orange → green):</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH</div>\n<p>Ketones are <strong>not</strong> oxidised: there's no H on the carbonyl carbon to remove.</p>\n\n<h2>Nucleophilic addition</h2>\n<h3>Reduction with NaBH₄</h3>\n<p><strong>Sodium tetrahydridoborate(III), NaBH₄</strong>, in aqueous solution, reduces carbonyls to alcohols. The reducing agent is written as <strong>[H]</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">aldehyde → primary alcohol: CH₃CHO + 2[H] → CH₃CH₂OH<br>ketone → secondary alcohol: CH₃COCH₃ + 2[H] → CH₃CH(OH)CH₃</div>\n<h3>Addition of HCN</h3>\n<p>Using <strong>NaCN and H₂SO₄</strong> (which make HCN in the flask; HCN itself is a very toxic gas), carbonyls form <strong>hydroxynitriles</strong>. This adds a carbon to the chain (see 6.2.4).</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CHO + HCN → CH₃CH(OH)CN &nbsp;&nbsp;(2-hydroxypropanenitrile)</div>\n<h3>The mechanism</h3>\n<p>The nucleophile is the <strong>hydride ion, H⁻</strong> (from NaBH₄) or the <strong>cyanide ion, CN⁻</strong>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A curly arrow from the <strong>lone pair</strong> on the nucleophile (H⁻ or the carbon of :CN⁻) to the <strong>δ+ carbon</strong> of C=O.</li>\n<li>At the same time a curly arrow from the <strong>C=O π-bond</strong> to the oxygen, giving an <strong>intermediate</strong> with O⁻.</li>\n<li>A curly arrow from a <strong>lone pair on O⁻</strong> to an H of water (or H⁺), forming the –OH; with water, OH⁻ is released.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When CN⁻ adds to an aldehyde (other than methanal) or an unsymmetrical ketone, the planar C=O can be attacked from either side equally, so a <strong>racemic mixture</strong> of optical isomers forms (6.2.2).</p>\n\n<h2>Tests</h2>\n<h3>2,4-DNP: any carbonyl</h3>\n<p>Add <strong>2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine</strong> (Brady's reagent). An <strong>orange precipitate</strong> shows an aldehyde or ketone. Carboxylic acids and esters don't react. The precipitate can be <strong>recrystallised</strong> and its <strong>melting point</strong> measured and compared with known values to identify exactly which carbonyl compound it came from.</p>\n<h3>Tollens' reagent: aldehydes only</h3>\n<p><strong>Tollens' reagent</strong> is ammoniacal silver nitrate (Ag⁺ in aqueous ammonia). Warm it with the compound in a water bath. An <strong>aldehyde</strong> gives a <strong>silver mirror</strong> (Ag⁺ is reduced to Ag while the aldehyde is oxidised to a carboxylic acid). A ketone gives no change.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag</div>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> in the nucleophilic addition mechanism, the dipole on C=O must be shown, the first arrow must start from the lone pair (on the C of CN⁻ or on H⁻), and the intermediate must carry a negative charge on oxygen.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Reactivity of the carbonyl group</h2>\n<p>Unlike the C=C in alkenes, the C=O bond is polar. The oxygen pulls electron density toward itself, leaving the carbon δ+. So carbonyl compounds are attacked by <strong>nucleophiles</strong> (electron-pair donors) at carbon, not by electrophiles. That's why bromine water doesn't react with aldehydes and ketones in the way it does with alkenes.</p>\n<h2>Reactions in summary</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Reagent</th><th>Aldehyde</th><th>Ketone</th></tr>\n<tr><td>K₂Cr₂O₇ / H₂SO₄, reflux</td><td>carboxylic acid (orange → green)</td><td>no reaction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tollens' reagent, warm</td><td>silver mirror</td><td>no reaction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NaBH₄(aq)</td><td>primary alcohol</td><td>secondary alcohol</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NaCN / H₂SO₄</td><td>hydroxynitrile</td><td>hydroxynitrile</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2,4-DNP</td><td>orange precipitate</td><td>orange precipitate</td></tr></table></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Name the products when propanal and propanone each react with NaBH₄, and with HCN.</p>\n<p>NaBH₄: propanal → <strong>propan-1-ol</strong>; propanone → <strong>propan-2-ol</strong>.</p>\n<p>HCN: propanal → <strong>2-hydroxybutanenitrile</strong> CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CN; propanone → <strong>2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile</strong> (CH₃)₂C(OH)CN. Both gain a carbon.</p></div>\n<h2>Writing the NaBH₄ equation</h2>\n<p>Use [H] for the reducing agent: an aldehyde or ketone gains two hydrogens. CH₃COCH₂CH₃ + 2[H] → CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ (butanone → butan-2-ol).</p>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is HCN generated in the flask from NaCN and H₂SO₄ rather than used directly?</summary><div><p>HCN is an extremely toxic gas; making it in solution as needed reduces the risk (the reaction is done in a fume cupboard).</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>A compound gives an orange precipitate with 2,4-DNP but no silver mirror with Tollens’ reagent. What is it?</summary><div><p>A ketone.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What does Tollens’ reagent contain, and what happens to it with an aldehyde?</summary><div><p>Ag⁺ in aqueous ammonia; Ag⁺ is reduced to silver metal (Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag) while the aldehyde is oxidised.</p></div></details>\n<h2>More to try</h2><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Give the structure of the product of butanone with HCN.</summary><div><p>CH₃C(OH)(CN)CH₂CH₃, 2-hydroxy-2-methylbutanenitrile.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How could you use 2,4-DNP to identify which ketone you have?</summary><div><p>Recrystallise the orange precipitate, measure its melting point and compare it with known values for 2,4-DNP derivatives.</p></div></details>\n<h2>AQA aldehyde and ketone details</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tollens’ reagent</strong> ([Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺): warm with an aldehyde and a silver mirror forms. <strong>Fehling’s solution</strong> (blue Cu²⁺): warm with an aldehyde and a brick-red precipitate of Cu₂O forms. Ketones give no change with either.</li>\n<li><strong>NaBH₄</strong> in aqueous solution reduces aldehydes to primary alcohols and ketones to secondary alcohols. Use [H] in equations: CH₃CHO + 2[H] → CH₃CH₂OH. The nucleophile is the hydride ion, H⁻.</li>\n<li><strong>KCN then dilute acid</strong>: nucleophilic addition of CN⁻ to make a hydroxynitrile. KCN is used instead of HCN because HCN is a very toxic gas; KCN is still highly toxic, so the reaction is done in a fume cupboard.</li>\n<li>Attack can happen from either side of the planar C=O, so an unsymmetrical aldehyde or ketone gives a <strong>racemic mixture</strong> of the two enantiomers.</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Nucleophilic addition","A nucleophile attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon and the C=O π bond breaks; H⁺ then adds to O⁻."]],"qa":[["Reducing agent for aldehydes and ketones","NaBH₄ in aqueous solution (hydride ion H⁻)."],["Reduction product of an aldehyde and a ketone","Primary alcohol and secondary alcohol."],["Carbonyl + HCN","Hydroxynitrile; use NaCN and H₂SO₄ (HCN is toxic); extends the carbon chain by one."],["Test for a carbonyl group","2,4-DNP (Brady’s reagent): orange precipitate; melting point of the purified derivative identifies the compound."],["Test to distinguish an aldehyde from a ketone","Tollens’ reagent: silver mirror with an aldehyde; no change with a ketone."],["Oxidation of an aldehyde","Acidified dichromate under reflux gives a carboxylic acid."],["Why is the carbonyl carbon attacked by nucleophiles?","O is more electronegative, so C=O is polar and the carbon is δ+."],["Mechanism of NaBH₄ reduction","Nucleophilic addition: H⁻ attacks the δ+ carbon, then the O⁻ intermediate is protonated by water."],["What does Tollens’ reagent contain?","Ammoniacal silver nitrate: [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺. Ag⁺ is reduced to Ag (silver mirror)."],["Why is HCN made in situ from NaCN and acid?","HCN is a very toxic gas."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which reagent reduces propanone to propan-2-ol?","o":["Acidified K₂Cr₂O₇","NaBH₄","Tollens’ reagent","KCN"],"a":1,"e":"NaBH₄ provides H⁻ ions that add to the C=O (nucleophilic addition)."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Aldehydes and Ketones","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.08.%20Aldehydes%20and%20Ketones.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Aldehydes and Ketones - Properties, Reactions, Identification and 2,4-DNP","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.08.%20Aldehydes%20and%20Ketones%20-%20Properties,%20Reactions,%20Identification%20and%202,4-DNP.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Aldehydes and Ketones","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.08.%20Aldehydes%20and%20Ketones.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.9","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Carboxylic acids and derivatives","short":"Acids, esters, hydrolysis, biodiesel, acyl chlorides and acid anhydrides.","spec":["Carboxylic acids are weak acids that react with carbonates to release CO₂","Esterification with alcohols and an acid catalyst; uses of esters","Acid and alkaline hydrolysis of esters; fats and oils, soap, glycerol and biodiesel","Acylation: acyl chlorides and acid anhydrides react with water, alcohols, ammonia and primary amines","Nucleophilic addition–elimination mechanism for acyl chlorides","Industrial advantages of ethanoic anhydride for making aspirin; required practical 10: preparation of an organic solid and an organic liquid"],"notes":"\n<h2>Carboxylic acids</h2>\n<p>Carboxylic acids contain the <strong>carboxyl group, –COOH</strong>. Named -oic acid: methanoic acid HCOOH, ethanoic acid CH₃COOH, benzoic acid C₆H₅COOH. Dicarboxylic acids: ethanedioic acid HOOC–COOH.</p>\n<h3>Solubility</h3>\n<p>The C=O and O–H groups both form <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong> with water, so small carboxylic acids are very soluble. Solubility falls as the non-polar chain gets longer.</p>\n<h3>Acidity</h3>\n<p>Carboxylic acids are <strong>weak acids</strong>: CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺. They show all the typical acid reactions (redox with metals, neutralisation with bases):</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">2CH₃COOH + Mg → (CH₃COO)₂Mg + H₂<br>CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O<br>2CH₃COOH + CaO → (CH₃COO)₂Ca + H₂O<br>2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂</div>\n<p>The reaction with carbonates (<strong>effervescence</strong>) is the test for a carboxylic acid: phenols and alcohols don't react with carbonates. Salts are named -oate: sodium ethanoate.</p>\n\n<h2>Esters</h2>\n<p>Esters contain the group –COO–. They are named from the alcohol (as an alkyl group) then the acid (-oate): CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ is <strong>ethyl ethanoate</strong> (from ethanol and ethanoic acid). Esters have sweet, fruity smells and are used in perfumes, flavourings and as solvents.</p>\n<h3>Making esters (esterification)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Carboxylic acid + alcohol</strong>, with a <strong>concentrated sulfuric acid</strong> catalyst, warmed. Reversible, so the yield is limited:<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃COOH + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + H₂O</div></li>\n<li><strong>Acid anhydride + alcohol</strong>, warmed. Not reversible, so a better yield:<div class=\"rxn\">(CH₃CO)₂O + CH₃OH → CH₃COOCH₃ + CH₃COOH</div>An acid anhydride is two carboxylic acid molecules joined with the loss of water.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Hydrolysis of esters</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acid hydrolysis</strong>: heat under reflux with dilute acid. The reverse of esterification, so it's <strong>reversible</strong>: products are the carboxylic acid and alcohol.</li>\n<li><strong>Alkaline hydrolysis</strong>: heat under reflux with aqueous NaOH. <strong>Not reversible</strong>; products are the <strong>carboxylate salt</strong> and the alcohol. This is how soap is made from fats.<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + NaOH → CH₃COONa + CH₃CH₂OH</div></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Acyl chlorides</h2>\n<p><strong>Acyl chlorides</strong> contain –COCl, named -oyl chloride: ethanoyl chloride CH₃COCl. They are made from carboxylic acids with <strong>thionyl chloride, SOCl₂</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃COOH + SOCl₂ → CH₃COCl + SO₂ + HCl</div>\n<p>Both by-products are gases, which makes the acyl chloride easy to purify. Acyl chlorides are <strong>very reactive</strong> (the carbonyl carbon is bonded to two electronegative atoms, O and Cl, so it's strongly δ+). They react vigorously and irreversibly, giving misty fumes of HCl:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Reacts with</th><th>Product</th><th>Equation</th></tr>\n<tr><td>water</td><td>carboxylic acid</td><td>CH₃COCl + H₂O → CH₃COOH + HCl</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alcohol</td><td>ester</td><td>CH₃COCl + CH₃OH → CH₃COOCH₃ + HCl</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ammonia</td><td>primary amide</td><td>CH₃COCl + 2NH₃ → CH₃CONH₂ + NH₄Cl</td></tr>\n<tr><td>primary amine</td><td>secondary amide</td><td>CH₃COCl + 2CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₃ + CH₃NH₃Cl</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>With ammonia and amines, two moles are used: the second neutralises the HCl formed.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> an ester's name comes from the alcohol first, but its formula usually puts the acid part first: methyl propanoate is CH₃CH₂COOCH₃. Find the C=O: the side with the C=O came from the acid.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Comparing acidity</h2>\n<p>Order of acidity: <strong>carboxylic acids > phenols > water > alcohols</strong>. Only carboxylic acids are strong enough to react with carbonates. Phenols react with NaOH but not with carbonates. Alcohols don't react with either.</p>\n<h2>Improving ester yield</h2>\n<p>Esterification from an acid and alcohol is reversible with K<sub>c</sub> about 4, so the yield is limited. It can be improved by using an excess of the cheaper reactant, removing the ester by distillation as it forms, or using an acyl chloride or acid anhydride instead (not reversible).</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Name and give the formula of the ester made from propanoic acid and methanol, and write the equation.</p>\n<p>CH₃CH₂COOH + CH₃OH ⇌ CH₃CH₂COOCH₃ + H₂O. <strong>Methyl propanoate</strong>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Give the products of hydrolysing ethyl butanoate with (a) dilute H₂SO₄, (b) NaOH(aq).</p>\n<p>(a) <strong>Butanoic acid and ethanol</strong> (reversible). (b) <strong>Sodium butanoate and ethanol</strong> (goes to completion).</p></div>\n<h2>Why acyl chlorides are so reactive</h2>\n<p>The carbonyl carbon is attached to both O and Cl, both highly electronegative, so it carries a large δ+ charge and is attacked easily by nucleophiles (water, alcohols, ammonia, amines). Cl⁻ is a good leaving group. The reactions are fast at room temperature and give HCl as a by-product.</p>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Name CH₃CH₂COOCH₂CH₃.</summary><div><p>Ethyl propanoate.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the equation for ethanoyl chloride with ethylamine.</summary><div><p>CH₃COCl + 2CH₃CH₂NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₂CH₃ + CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺Cl⁻ (N-ethylethanamide).</p></div></details>\n<h2>Fats, oils, soap and biodiesel</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vegetable oils and animal fats are esters of <strong>propane-1,2,3-triol</strong> (glycerol) with long-chain fatty acids.</li>\n<li>Hydrolysis with hot NaOH (saponification) gives glycerol and the sodium salts of the fatty acids: <strong>soap</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Biodiesel</strong> is a mixture of methyl esters of fatty acids, made by reacting vegetable oils with methanol and a catalyst (e.g. KOH).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Acid anhydrides</h2>\n<p>Acid anhydrides, such as ethanoic anhydride (CH₃CO)₂O, react like acyl chlorides but more slowly, and they give a carboxylic acid instead of HCl:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>with water: (CH₃CO)₂O + H₂O → 2CH₃COOH</li>\n<li>with alcohols: (CH₃CO)₂O + CH₃OH → CH₃COOCH₃ + CH₃COOH</li>\n<li>with ammonia: (CH₃CO)₂O + 2NH₃ → CH₃CONH₂ + CH₃COONH₄</li>\n<li>with primary amines: (CH₃CO)₂O + 2CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₃ + CH₃COO⁻CH₃NH₃⁺</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ethanoic anhydride is used to make <strong>aspirin</strong> industrially instead of ethanoyl chloride because it is cheaper, less corrosive, reacts less violently with water and does not make corrosive HCl fumes.</p>\n<h2>Nucleophilic addition–elimination</h2>\n<p>The nucleophile’s lone pair attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon and the C=O electrons move onto oxygen (addition). The C=O then reforms and Cl⁻ is expelled; finally, Cl⁻ (or another base) removes H⁺ from the nucleophile (elimination). You need this mechanism for acyl chlorides with water, alcohols, ammonia and primary amines.</p>\n","terms":[["Ester","A compound with the –COO– functional group, made from a carboxylic acid (or derivative) and an alcohol."],["Acyl chloride","Compound with the –COCl group; very reactive."],["Acid anhydride","Compound with –COOCO–, formed from two carboxylic acid molecules."],["Biodiesel","A mixture of methyl esters of long-chain fatty acids, made from vegetable oils and methanol."]],"qa":[["Why are short-chain carboxylic acids soluble in water?","The C=O and O–H groups hydrogen bond with water."],["Carboxylic acid + carbonate","Salt + water + CO₂: effervescence (test for –COOH)."],["Esterification: conditions","Carboxylic acid + alcohol, concentrated H₂SO₄ catalyst, heat."],["Acid hydrolysis of an ester","Heat under reflux with dilute acid: carboxylic acid + alcohol (reversible)."],["Alkaline hydrolysis of an ester","Heat under reflux with NaOH(aq): carboxylate salt + alcohol (goes to completion)."],["Making an acyl chloride","Carboxylic acid + SOCl₂ → acyl chloride + SO₂ + HCl."],["Acyl chloride + water / alcohol / ammonia / amine","Carboxylic acid; ester; primary amide; secondary amide (each + HCl)."],["Why is ethanoic anhydride used to make aspirin instead of ethanoyl chloride?","It is cheaper, less corrosive, reacts less violently and does not give off HCl fumes."],["What forms when a fat is hydrolysed by NaOH?","Glycerol (propane-1,2,3-triol) and soap (the sodium salts of the fatty acids)."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Ethanoyl chloride reacts with methylamine. What is the organic product?","o":["N-methylethanamide","Methyl ethanoate","Ethanamide","Ethylamine"],"a":0,"e":"Nucleophilic addition–elimination gives the N-substituted amide CH₃CONHCH₃."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":["rp10"],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Carboxylic Acids and Esters","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.09.%20Carboxylic%20Acids%20and%20Esters.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Carboxylic Acids, Esters and Derivatives","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.09.%20Carboxylic%20Acids,%20Esters%20and%20Derivatives.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Friedel-Crafts Reactions - Alkylation and Acylation","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.09.%20Friedel-Crafts%20Reactions%20-%20Alkylation%20and%20Acylation.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Carboxylic Acids and Derivatives","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.09.%20Carboxylic%20Acids%20and%20Derivatives.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"},{"set":"Required practical guides","label":"RP 10: Preparation of organic solids and liquids","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Practical-Skills/RP%2010%20-%20Preparation%20of%20organic%20solids%20and%20liquids.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.10","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Aromatic chemistry","short":"Benzene’s structure and stability, and electrophilic substitution.","spec":["The delocalised structure of benzene; evidence from enthalpies of hydrogenation for its extra stability","Electrophilic substitution mechanisms: nitration (with the formation of NO₂⁺) and Friedel–Crafts acylation with AlCl₃","Nitration is important for making explosives and amines"],"notes":"\n<h2>The structure of benzene</h2>\n<p>Benzene, C₆H₆, is a planar hexagonal ring of six carbon atoms, each bonded to one hydrogen. Two models have been used to describe it.</p>\n<h3>The Kekulé model</h3>\n<p>Kekulé (1865) proposed a ring of alternating single and double C=C bonds (cyclohexa-1,3,5-triene). Three pieces of evidence show this is wrong:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bond lengths</strong>: X-ray diffraction shows all six C–C bonds are the <strong>same length</strong> (0.139 nm), between a single C–C (0.153 nm) and a double C=C (0.134 nm). Kekulé's model predicts two different lengths.</li>\n<li><strong>Enthalpy of hydrogenation</strong>: hydrogenating cyclohexene (one C=C) releases −120 kJ mol⁻¹, so Kekulé's structure should release 3 × −120 = −360 kJ mol⁻¹. Benzene actually releases only <strong>−208 kJ mol⁻¹</strong>: it is about 150 kJ mol⁻¹ <strong>more stable</strong> than predicted.</li>\n<li><strong>Resistance to reaction</strong>: benzene does <strong>not decolourise bromine water</strong> and doesn't easily undergo addition, unlike alkenes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The delocalised model</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Each carbon uses three electrons in σ-bonds (two to neighbouring carbons, one to H), forming a planar ring with 120° bond angles.</li>\n<li>Each carbon has one electron left in a <strong>p orbital</strong> above and below the plane.</li>\n<li>The p orbitals <strong>overlap sideways</strong> with both neighbours, forming a ring of <strong>delocalised π-electrons</strong> above and below the plane of the ring (six electrons, spread over all six carbons).</li>\n<li>So all C–C bonds are identical, and the delocalisation makes benzene unusually stable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Draw benzene as a hexagon with a circle inside. In naming, benzene is the parent (chlorobenzene, nitrobenzene, methylbenzene); with some groups it becomes a substituent called <strong>phenyl</strong> (phenylamine C₆H₅NH₂, phenol C₆H₅OH; benzoic acid is C₆H₅COOH).</p>\n\n<h2>Electrophilic substitution</h2>\n<p>Benzene's delocalised π-electrons attract electrophiles, but addition would destroy the stable delocalised system. So benzene reacts by <strong>electrophilic substitution</strong>: an H atom is replaced, and the delocalised ring is restored.</p>\n<h3>The general mechanism</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>The electrophile (E⁺) is generated.</li>\n<li>A curly arrow from the <strong>delocalised ring</strong> to E⁺: a new C–E bond forms, giving an intermediate with a broken ring (drawn as a horseshoe with a + inside, opening toward the sp³ carbon).</li>\n<li>A curly arrow from the <strong>C–H bond</strong> back into the ring: H⁺ is lost and the delocalised ring is restored.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Nitration</h3>\n<p>Benzene + <strong>concentrated nitric acid</strong> with <strong>concentrated sulfuric acid</strong> catalyst, at <strong>50 °C</strong> (or below, to avoid further nitration):</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">C₆H₆ + HNO₃ → C₆H₅NO₂ + H₂O</div>\n<p>The electrophile is the <strong>nitronium ion, NO₂⁺</strong>: HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + HSO₄⁻ + H₂O. Then H⁺ + HSO₄⁻ → H₂SO₄ regenerates the catalyst. Nitrobenzene is used to make dyes (via phenylamine) and explosives.</p>\n<h3>Halogenation</h3>\n<p>Benzene doesn't react with Br₂ alone. It needs a <strong>halogen carrier</strong> catalyst such as AlCl₃, FeCl₃, AlBr₃ or FeBr₃ (or iron filings, which react with the halogen to form one), at room temperature:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">C₆H₆ + Br₂ → C₆H₅Br + HBr</div>\n<p>The halogen carrier makes the electrophile: Br₂ + FeBr₃ → <strong>Br⁺</strong> + FeBr₄⁻. After substitution: H⁺ + FeBr₄⁻ → FeBr₃ + HBr.</p>\n<h3>Friedel–Crafts reactions</h3>\n<p>These form <strong>C–C bonds</strong> to a benzene ring, using a halogen carrier (AlCl₃) catalyst:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alkylation</strong>: with a halogenoalkane. C₆H₆ + CH₃CH₂Cl → C₆H₅CH₂CH₃ + HCl. Electrophile: CH₃CH₂⁺ (from RCl + AlCl₃ → R⁺ + AlCl₄⁻).</li>\n<li><strong>Acylation</strong>: with an <strong>acyl chloride</strong>, forming an aromatic ketone. C₆H₆ + CH₃COCl → C₆H₅COCH₃ + HCl. Electrophile: CH₃CO⁺ (acylium ion).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Why benzene resists bromine where alkenes don't</h3>\n<p>In an alkene the π-electrons are <strong>localised</strong> between two carbons: a region of high electron density that polarises Br₂ enough to react. In benzene the π-electrons are <strong>delocalised</strong> over six carbons, so the electron density at any one bond is lower: it can't polarise Br₂ enough. A halogen carrier is needed to make a stronger electrophile.</p>\n\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write an equation to show the formation of the nitronium ion.</summary><div><p>HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + HSO₄⁻ + H₂O</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What is the role of FeBr₃ in the bromination of benzene?</summary><div><p>A halogen carrier: it reacts with Br₂ to form the electrophile Br⁺ (and FeBr₄⁻), and is regenerated.</p></div></details>\n<h2>AQA aromatic details</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evidence for delocalisation</strong>: the enthalpy of hydrogenation of benzene (−208 kJ mol⁻¹) is much less exothermic than three times that of cyclohexene (3 × −120 = −360 kJ mol⁻¹). Benzene is about 152 kJ mol⁻¹ more stable than the Kekulé structure suggests. All C–C bonds are the same length, between single and double.</li>\n<li><strong>Nitration</strong>: concentrated HNO₃ and concentrated H₂SO₄ at about 50 °C. The electrophile forms as HNO₃ + 2H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + H₃O⁺ + 2HSO₄⁻. Nitro compounds are used to make explosives (TNT) and are reduced to aromatic amines for dyes.</li>\n<li><strong>Friedel–Crafts acylation</strong>: an acyl chloride with an AlCl₃ catalyst, heated under reflux. The electrophile is RCO⁺: RCOCl + AlCl₃ → RCO⁺ + AlCl₄⁻. AlCl₃ is regenerated at the end: H⁺ + AlCl₄⁻ → AlCl₃ + HCl. The product is an aromatic ketone such as phenylethanone.</li>\n<li>Benzene resists addition, because addition would destroy the stable delocalised system.</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Electrophilic substitution","A reaction in which an electrophile replaces an atom (usually H) on an aromatic ring."],["Delocalised model of benzene","Each C has a p orbital that overlaps sideways with its neighbours to form a ring of π electron density above and below the plane."]],"qa":[["Three pieces of evidence against the Kekulé model","All C–C bonds are the same length (0.139 nm); benzene does not decolourise bromine water; ΔhydH is less exothermic than predicted (more stable)."],["Nitration of benzene: reagents and conditions","Concentrated HNO₃ and concentrated H₂SO₄, 50 °C."],["Formation of the nitronium ion","HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ + HSO₄⁻ + H₂O."],["Halogenation of benzene: conditions","Halogen with a halogen carrier: AlCl₃, FeCl₃ or FeBr₃ (or Fe)."],["Friedel–Crafts alkylation and acylation","Halogenoalkane or acyl chloride with AlCl₃ halogen carrier: forms a C–C bond to the ring."],["Why doesn’t benzene react with bromine readily?","Delocalised π electrons spread charge, so electron density is too low to polarise Br₂."],["Directing effects","–OH and –NH₂ direct to 2- and 4- positions; –NO₂ directs to the 3-position."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"What is the electrophile in the nitration of benzene?","o":["NO₂⁻","NO₃⁻","NO₂⁺","HNO₃"],"a":2,"e":"NO₂⁺ forms from concentrated HNO₃ and H₂SO₄."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Aromatic Chemistry","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.10.%20Aromatic%20Chemistry.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Benzene - Structure, Reactions and Substitution","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.10.%20Benzene%20-%20Structure,%20Reactions%20and%20Substitution.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Aromatic Chemistry","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.10.%20Aromatic%20Chemistry.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.11","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Amines","short":"Preparation, base strength and nucleophilic properties of amines.","spec":["Primary, secondary and tertiary amines and quaternary ammonium salts; uses of quaternary ammonium salts as cationic surfactants","Making aliphatic amines from halogenoalkanes with ammonia, and by reducing nitriles","Making aromatic amines by reducing nitro compounds (Sn/HCl)","Base strength of ammonia, primary aliphatic and primary aromatic amines, explained by the lone pair availability","Nucleophilic substitution with halogenoalkanes, and nucleophilic addition–elimination with acyl chlorides and acid anhydrides"],"notes":"\n<h2>Amines</h2>\n<p>Amines are derivatives of ammonia in which one or more H atoms are replaced by carbon chains or rings.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> amines: one carbon attached to N, –NH₂ (methylamine CH₃NH₂, ethylamine).</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong>: two carbons on N (dimethylamine (CH₃)₂NH).</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary</strong>: three carbons on N (trimethylamine (CH₃)₃N).</li>\n<li><strong>Aromatic</strong> amines: N attached to a benzene ring (<strong>phenylamine</strong>, C₆H₅NH₂).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Basicity</h3>\n<p>The nitrogen has a <strong>lone pair</strong> that can accept a proton, so amines are <strong>bases</strong> (Brønsted–Lowry proton acceptors), like ammonia.</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃NH₂ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃NH₃⁺ + OH⁻</div>\n<p>Amines react with acids to form <strong>salts</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH₂NH₂ + HCl → CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺Cl⁻ &nbsp;&nbsp;(ethylammonium chloride)<br>2CH₃NH₂ + H₂SO₄ → (CH₃NH₃⁺)₂SO₄²⁻</div>\n\n<h2>Making aliphatic amines</h2>\n<p>Heat a halogenoalkane with <strong>excess ethanolic ammonia</strong> (ammonia dissolved in ethanol; water would cause hydrolysis to an alcohol instead). Ammonia is the nucleophile:</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br</div>\n<p>The second NH₃ removes the H⁺ (as NH₄Br). The product amine also has a lone pair and can attack more halogenoalkane, forming secondary and tertiary amines and quaternary ammonium salts. <strong>Excess ammonia</strong> makes the primary amine the main product. Using a primary amine instead of ammonia gives a secondary amine.</p>\n\n<h2>Making aromatic amines</h2>\n<p>Phenylamine is made by reducing <strong>nitrobenzene</strong> with <strong>tin and concentrated hydrochloric acid</strong>, heated under reflux, followed by excess NaOH (the acidic conditions first give the salt, C₆H₅NH₃⁺; the alkali releases the amine):</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">C₆H₅NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅NH₂ + 2H₂O</div>\n<p>Phenylamine is used to make dyes. So benzene → nitrobenzene → phenylamine is a two-step route to aromatic amines.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> when an amine reacts with an acid, the product is a salt, not a covalent compound. Draw the N with four bonds and a + charge, and the anion separately.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>How basic are amines?</h2>\n<p>All amines are bases because of the lone pair on nitrogen. Aliphatic amines are slightly stronger bases than ammonia: alkyl groups push electron density onto the nitrogen, making its lone pair more available to accept a proton. Phenylamine is a much weaker base: the nitrogen's lone pair is partly delocalised into the benzene ring, so it is less available.</p>\n<h2>The mechanism with ammonia</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>The lone pair on NH₃ attacks the δ+ carbon of the halogenoalkane; the C–X bond breaks and X⁻ leaves.</li>\n<li>This gives an ammonium ion, RNH₃⁺.</li>\n<li>A second NH₃ molecule removes H⁺ from RNH₃⁺, giving the amine RNH₂ and NH₄⁺.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because the amine product also has a lone pair, it can react with more halogenoalkane: RNH₂ + RX → R₂NH + HX, and so on to R₃N and R₄N⁺X⁻ (a quaternary ammonium salt, used in fabric softeners and detergents). Excess ammonia makes it most likely that each halogenoalkane meets an NH₃ molecule rather than an amine.</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Suggest how to make propylamine from 1-bromopropane, and name one by-product that might form.</p>\n<p>Heat 1-bromopropane with <strong>excess ammonia in ethanol</strong>: CH₃CH₂CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br.</p>\n<p>By-product: dipropylamine, (CH₃CH₂CH₂)₂NH, from further substitution.</p></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Give a two-step route from benzene to phenylamine.</p>\n<p>1. Conc. HNO₃ / conc. H₂SO₄, 50 °C → nitrobenzene. 2. Sn / conc. HCl, heat under reflux, then NaOH(aq) → <strong>phenylamine</strong>.</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is ethanolic rather than aqueous ammonia used to make amines from halogenoalkanes?</summary><div><p>In water, hydroxide ions (and water) would substitute instead, forming an alcohol.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the equation for methylamine reacting with hydrochloric acid.</summary><div><p>CH₃NH₂ + HCl → CH₃NH₃⁺Cl⁻ (methylammonium chloride).</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Classify (CH₃)₂NH.</summary><div><p>A secondary amine (dimethylamine).</p></div></details>\n<h2>Amines and amides: telling them apart</h2>\n<p>An amine has N bonded only to C and H; an amide has N bonded to a C=O. Amides are <strong>not</strong> basic in the same way, because the lone pair on N is partly delocalised onto the C=O. In naming, the group on N in a secondary amide is shown with \"N-\": CH₃CONHCH₃ is N-methylethanamide.</p>\n<h2>More to try</h2><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Name (CH₃CH₂)₂NH and classify it.</summary><div><p>Diethylamine; a secondary amine.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Write the equation for the reduction of nitrobenzene, using [H].</summary><div><p>C₆H₅NO₂ + 6[H] → C₆H₅NH₂ + 2H₂O</p></div></details>\n<h2>AQA amine details</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Quaternary ammonium salts</strong>, R₄N⁺X⁻, form when a tertiary amine reacts further with a halogenoalkane. Those with long carbon chains are <strong>cationic surfactants</strong>, used in fabric softeners and hair conditioners.</li>\n<li><strong>Making primary amines</strong>: halogenoalkane + excess ammonia (gives a mixture of products, so separation is needed), or reduce a nitrile with LiAlH₄ or with H₂ and a nickel catalyst (gives only the primary amine: better for industry).</li>\n<li><strong>Aromatic amines</strong>: reduce a nitro compound with tin and concentrated HCl, then add NaOH. Used to make dyes.</li>\n<li><strong>Base strength</strong>: primary aliphatic amines are stronger bases than ammonia because the alkyl group pushes electron density onto the nitrogen, making its lone pair more available. Phenylamine is weaker than ammonia because the lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring.</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Amine","A compound derived from ammonia where one or more H atoms are replaced by carbon chains or rings."],["Primary, secondary and tertiary amines","N bonded to one, two or three carbon groups."],["Quaternary ammonium salt","A salt R₄N⁺X⁻ with four alkyl groups on nitrogen; long-chain ones are cationic surfactants."]],"qa":[["Why do amines act as bases?","The N lone pair accepts a proton."],["Amine + acid","A salt, e.g. CH₃NH₂ + HCl → CH₃NH₃⁺Cl⁻."],["Making a primary aliphatic amine","Halogenoalkane + excess ethanolic ammonia (excess reduces further substitution)."],["Making a secondary amine","Halogenoalkane + a primary amine in ethanol."],["Making an aromatic amine","Reduce a nitroarene with tin and concentrated HCl, then add NaOH."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which is the strongest base?","o":["Ammonia","Phenylamine","Ethylamine","Water"],"a":2,"e":"The ethyl group pushes electron density onto N, making its lone pair more available. In phenylamine the lone pair is delocalised into the ring."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Amines","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.11.%20Amines.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Amines, Amino Acids, Amides and Peptides","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.11.%20Amines,%20Amino%20Acids,%20Amides%20and%20Peptides.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Amines","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.11.%20Amines.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.12","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Polymers","short":"Condensation polymers: polyesters and polyamides, hydrolysis and disposal.","spec":["Condensation polymers: polyesters (e.g. Terylene), polyamides (e.g. nylon 6,6 and Kevlar) and polypeptides","Linkages and repeating units from monomers, and monomers from polymer sections","Hydrogen bonding between polyamide chains","Polyalkenes are chemically inert and non-biodegradable; polyesters and polyamides can be hydrolysed and are biodegradable","Advantages and disadvantages of landfill, incineration and recycling"],"notes":"\n<h2>Condensation polymerisation</h2>\n<p>In <strong>condensation polymerisation</strong>, monomers with <strong>two functional groups</strong> each react together, and a <strong>small molecule</strong> (usually H₂O, or HCl if an acyl chloride is used) is lost each time a link forms. This contrasts with addition polymerisation (4.1.3), where alkenes join and nothing is lost.</p>\n<h3>Polyesters</h3>\n<p>Formed by joining monomers with <strong>ester links</strong>, –COO–:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>dicarboxylic acid</strong> + a <strong>diol</strong>, e.g. benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid + ethane-1,2-diol → <strong>PET (Terylene)</strong>, used in drinks bottles and clothing fibres;</li>\n<li>or a single monomer with <strong>both groups</strong> (a hydroxycarboxylic acid), e.g. lactic acid (2-hydroxypropanoic acid) → <strong>poly(lactic acid)</strong>, a biodegradable polymer.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"rxn\">n HOOC–C₆H₄–COOH + n HO–CH₂CH₂–OH → –[OC–C₆H₄–COO–CH₂CH₂–O]ₙ– + 2n H₂O</div>\n<h3>Polyamides</h3>\n<p>Formed by joining monomers with <strong>amide links</strong>, –CONH–:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>dicarboxylic acid</strong> (or diacyl dichloride) + a <strong>diamine</strong>, e.g. hexanedioic acid + 1,6-diaminohexane → <strong>nylon-6,6</strong>;</li>\n<li>benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid + benzene-1,4-diamine → <strong>Kevlar</strong> (very strong, used in body armour);</li>\n<li>or <strong>amino acids</strong>, which have both groups: they join by amide (peptide) links to make <strong>proteins</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Drawing repeat units</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw the two monomers side by side.</li>\n<li>Remove –OH from the acid end and –H from the alcohol or amine end (these make H₂O).</li>\n<li>Join them with the ester or amide link and put square brackets with extension bonds around one unit of each monomer.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>To find monomers from a polymer, break each ester or amide link and add back –OH to the C=O side and –H to the O or N side.</p>\n\n<h2>Hydrolysis of condensation polymers</h2>\n<p>Ester and amide links can be broken by hydrolysis, so condensation polymers are generally <strong>biodegradable</strong> (unlike addition polymers).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polyesters</strong>: <strong>acid</strong> hydrolysis gives the dicarboxylic acid and diol; <strong>alkaline</strong> hydrolysis (NaOH) gives the <strong>carboxylate salt</strong> and diol.</li>\n<li><strong>Polyamides</strong>: <strong>acid</strong> hydrolysis gives the dicarboxylic acid and the <strong>ammonium salt</strong> of the diamine (the –NH₂ is protonated); <strong>alkaline</strong> hydrolysis gives the <strong>carboxylate salt</strong> and the diamine.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the products of hydrolysis depend on the conditions. In acid, amines come out as –NH₃⁺ salts; in alkali, acids come out as –COO⁻ salts. Check you've drawn the right form for the reagent given.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Recognising the type of polymer</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Main chain of only carbon atoms (C–C–C), side groups hanging off: <strong>addition polymer</strong> (from alkenes).</li>\n<li>–COO– links in the main chain: <strong>polyester</strong>.</li>\n<li>–CONH– links in the main chain: <strong>polyamide</strong> (or a protein).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Nylon-6,6 is made from hexanedioic acid, HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH, and 1,6-diaminohexane, H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂. Draw the repeat unit.</p>\n<p>Remove OH from each acid end and H from each amine end, then join: <strong>–[OC(CH₂)₄CO–NH(CH₂)₆NH]–</strong>. Water is lost at each link (2 per repeat unit).</p></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A polyester has the repeat unit –[OCH(CH₃)CO]–. Identify the monomer.</p>\n<p>Add OH to the C=O end and H to the O end: <strong>HOCH(CH₃)COOH, 2-hydroxypropanoic acid (lactic acid)</strong>. The polymer is poly(lactic acid).</p></div>\n<h2>Advantages of condensation polymers</h2>\n<p>Because they can be hydrolysed, polyesters and polyamides can be broken down by microorganisms or chemically recycled back to monomers. Poly(lactic acid) is made from renewable starch (corn), is biodegradable and compostable, and is used for packaging and dissolvable stitches. Kevlar's rigid chains and hydrogen bonding between chains make it extremely strong.</p>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What small molecule is lost when a diacyl dichloride reacts with a diamine?</summary><div><p>HCl.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Give the products of acid hydrolysis of nylon-6,6.</summary><div><p>Hexanedioic acid and the ammonium salt of 1,6-diaminohexane, ⁺H₃N(CH₂)₆NH₃⁺.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why are poly(alkenes) not biodegradable but polyesters can be?</summary><div><p>Poly(alkenes) have only strong, non-polar C–C and C–H bonds; polyesters have polar ester links that can be hydrolysed.</p></div></details>\n<h2>More to try</h2><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Is polyethene an addition or condensation polymer? How can you tell from its repeat unit?</summary><div><p>Addition: the main chain contains only C–C bonds, with no ester or amide links.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What are the monomers of PET (Terylene)?</summary><div><p>Benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid and ethane-1,2-diol.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Disposal of polymers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polyalkenes</strong> are non-polar and saturated, so they are chemically inert and <strong>non-biodegradable</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Polyesters and polyamides</strong> contain polar ester or amide links that can be hydrolysed, so they are biodegradable (slowly).</li>\n<li><strong>Landfill</strong>: cheap and easy, but uses land, and the polymers persist for years.</li>\n<li><strong>Incineration</strong>: releases energy that can generate electricity, but can produce toxic gases (e.g. HCl from PVC, which is removed by scrubbing) and adds CO₂ to the atmosphere.</li>\n<li><strong>Recycling</strong>: saves raw materials and energy and reduces landfill, but polymers must be collected and sorted, and quality can fall with each cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>Kevlar</strong> is a strong polyamide made from benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid and benzene-1,4-diamine; hydrogen bonds between chains give it great strength (body armour).</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Condensation polymerisation","Monomers join with the loss of a small molecule (usually water or HCl) at each link."],["Polyester","A condensation polymer linked by ester groups, from a dicarboxylic acid (or diacyl chloride) and a diol, or a hydroxycarboxylic acid."],["Polyamide","A condensation polymer linked by amide groups, from a dicarboxylic acid (or diacyl chloride) and a diamine, or an amino acid."]],"qa":[["Hydrolysis of polyesters and polyamides","Acid or alkali under reflux breaks the ester / amide links back into monomers (or their salts)."],["Why are condensation polymers more biodegradable than addition polymers?","Polar ester and amide links can be hydrolysed; addition polymers have non-polar C–C backbones."],["Product of alkaline hydrolysis of a polyamide","Dicarboxylate salt and a diamine."],["Product of acid hydrolysis of a polyamide","A dicarboxylic acid and the ammonium salt of the diamine."],["Small molecule lost when a diacyl chloride is used instead of a dicarboxylic acid","HCl instead of H₂O."],["How do you identify the monomers from a condensation polymer?","Break each ester or amide link; add –OH to the C=O end and –H to the O or N end."],["Name the link in a polyester and a polyamide","Ester link –COO–; amide (peptide) link –CONH–."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Why are polyalkenes non-biodegradable?","o":["They contain ester links","Their C–C and C–H bonds are non-polar and unreactive","They are condensation polymers","They are soluble in water"],"a":1,"e":"Polyalkenes are saturated and non-polar, so they are chemically inert."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Polymerisation","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.12.%20Polymerisation.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Polymers, Polyesters, Polyamides and Peptides","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.12.%20Polymers,%20Polyesters,%20Polyamides%20and%20Peptides.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Condensation Polymerisation","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.12.%20Condensation%20Polymerisation.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.13","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Amino acids, proteins and DNA","short":"Amino acids, protein structure, enzymes, DNA and the anticancer drug cisplatin.","spec":["Amino acids have acidic and basic properties and form zwitterions","Proteins are sequences of amino acids joined by peptide links; hydrolysis to amino acids","Primary, secondary (α-helix and β-pleated sheet) and tertiary structure: hydrogen bonds, sulfur–sulfur bonds and ionic interactions","Thin-layer chromatography and Rf values to identify amino acids","Enzymes: active sites, stereospecificity and inhibitors as drugs","DNA: nucleotides of a phosphate, 2-deoxyribose and a base (A, C, G, T); base pairing by hydrogen bonds","Cisplatin as an anticancer drug: binding to DNA, and its side effects"],"notes":"<h2>Amino acids</h2>\n<p>An <strong>α-amino acid</strong> has an amine group and a carboxylic acid group attached to the <strong>same carbon atom</strong>. General formula: <strong>RCH(NH₂)COOH</strong>, where R is a side chain that differs between amino acids (R = H in glycine, CH₃ in alanine).</p>\n<p>Because it has both a basic and an acidic group, an amino acid reacts with acids and with bases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>With an acid</strong>: the –NH₂ group is protonated. HOOCCH(R)NH₂ + HCl → HOOCCH(R)NH₃⁺Cl⁻.</li>\n<li><strong>With an alkali</strong>: the –COOH is neutralised. HOOCCH(R)NH₂ + NaOH → Na⁺⁻OOCCH(R)NH₂ + H₂O.</li>\n<li><strong>Esterification</strong>: with an alcohol and concentrated H₂SO₄ catalyst, the –COOH forms an ester (and in the acid, –NH₂ is protonated).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Amides</h2>\n<p>Amides contain –CONH–. <strong>Primary</strong> amides have –CONH₂ (ethanamide CH₃CONH₂). <strong>Secondary</strong> amides have one carbon on N (N-methylethanamide CH₃CONHCH₃). They are made from acyl chlorides and ammonia or amines (6.1.3). Amide links join amino acids in proteins and monomers in polyamides (6.2.3).</p>\n\n<h2>Amino acids in more depth</h2>\n<p>In solution, amino acids can exist as <strong>zwitterions</strong>: the –COOH donates H⁺ to the –NH₂ in the same molecule, giving ⁺H₃N–CHR–COO⁻, with both a positive and a negative charge. This is why amino acids are crystalline solids with high melting points (ionic attractions between zwitterions). You may meet zwitterions in context.</p>\n<p>Two amino acids join with an <strong>amide (peptide) link</strong>, –CONH–, losing water, to form a <strong>dipeptide</strong>. Two different amino acids can form two different dipeptides, depending on which –COOH reacts with which –NH₂.</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Glycine (H₂NCH₂COOH) and alanine (H₂NCH(CH₃)COOH) react. Give the two possible dipeptides.</p>\n<p>H₂NCH₂CO–NHCH(CH₃)COOH and H₂NCH(CH₃)CO–NHCH₂COOH.</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does glycine have no optical isomers?</summary><div><p>Its central carbon has two H atoms, so it does not have four different groups.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What is a racemic mixture?</summary><div><p>A 50 : 50 mixture of two optical isomers; it does not rotate plane-polarised light.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What does an amino acid form with NaOH(aq)?</summary><div><p>A sodium carboxylate salt: H₂NCH(R)COO⁻Na⁺ (and water).</p></div></details>\n<h2>Proteins</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Proteins are sequences of amino acids joined by <strong>peptide links</strong> (–CONH–) formed in condensation reactions.</li>\n<li>Hydrolysis with hot 6 mol dm⁻³ HCl breaks the peptide links to give the amino acids, which can be separated and identified by <strong>thin-layer chromatography</strong> and their R<sub>f</sub> values (located with ninhydrin or UV light).</li>\n<li><strong>Primary structure</strong>: the sequence of amino acids.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary structure</strong>: the α-helix and β-pleated sheet, held by <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong> between the C=O of one peptide link and the N–H of another.</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary structure</strong>: the overall 3D folding, held by hydrogen bonds, <strong>sulfur–sulfur (disulfide) bonds</strong> between cysteine residues and <strong>ionic interactions</strong> between charged side groups.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Enzymes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Enzymes are proteins that act as catalysts. The substrate binds to the <strong>active site</strong>, whose shape is complementary to it.</li>\n<li>The active site is <strong>stereospecific</strong>: it fits only one enantiomer of a chiral substrate.</li>\n<li>A drug can act as an <strong>inhibitor</strong> by binding to the active site and blocking the substrate. Computer modelling helps chemists design drug molecules with the right shape.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>DNA</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>DNA is a polymer of <strong>nucleotides</strong>. Each nucleotide has a phosphate group, the sugar <strong>2-deoxyribose</strong> and one of four bases: <strong>adenine (A), cytosine (C), guanine (G) and thymine (T)</strong>. Their structures are in the AQA Data Booklet.</li>\n<li>The sugar–phosphate backbone forms by condensation between the phosphate of one nucleotide and the sugar of the next.</li>\n<li>Two strands form a <strong>double helix</strong> held by <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong> between complementary base pairs: A with T (two hydrogen bonds) and C with G (three hydrogen bonds).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Cisplatin</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Cisplatin, cis-[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂], is a square planar anticancer drug.</li>\n<li>In the cell its chloride ligands are replaced, and the platinum bonds to <strong>nitrogen atoms on two adjacent guanine bases</strong> in DNA. This distorts the DNA and stops it replicating, so the cancer cells cannot divide.</li>\n<li>It also affects healthy cells that divide quickly (e.g. hair follicles), causing side effects such as hair loss. It is used in small doses for a short time to balance benefit and risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself (AQA extras)</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Which bonds hold the secondary structure of a protein together?</summary><div><p>Hydrogen bonds between the C=O of one peptide link and the N–H of another.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why does an enzyme catalyse the reaction of only one enantiomer?</summary><div><p>Its active site is stereospecific: only one enantiomer has the 3D shape that fits.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How does cisplatin stop cancer cells dividing?</summary><div><p>It binds to nitrogen atoms on two adjacent guanine bases in DNA, distorting the strand so DNA replication is prevented.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How many hydrogen bonds form between C and G in DNA?</summary><div><p>Three (A–T pairs form two).</p></div></details>\n","terms":[["α-amino acid","Contains NH₂ and COOH groups bonded to the same carbon: RCH(NH₂)COOH."],["Amide","Contains –CONH–; primary amides –CONH₂, secondary –CONHR."],["Primary structure","The sequence of amino acids in a protein chain."],["Secondary structure","The α-helix or β-pleated sheet, held by hydrogen bonds between peptide links."],["Tertiary structure","The overall 3D shape of a protein, held by hydrogen bonds, disulfide bonds and ionic interactions."],["Active site","The region of an enzyme where the substrate binds; its shape is stereospecific."],["Nucleotide","A monomer of DNA: a phosphate group, 2-deoxyribose and a base."],["Zwitterion","An ion with both a positive and a negative charge, such as ⁺H₃NCH(R)COO⁻."]],"qa":[["Amino acid + acid","The NH₂ group is protonated to –NH₃⁺."],["Amino acid + alkali","The COOH loses a proton to form –COO⁻ (salt)."],["Esterification of an amino acid","Heat with an alcohol and concentrated H₂SO₄."],["Which bases pair in DNA?","Adenine with thymine (2 hydrogen bonds); cytosine with guanine (3 hydrogen bonds)."],["Which sugar is in DNA?","2-deoxyribose."],["Which bonds form between cysteine residues?","Disulfide (S–S) bonds."],["What does cisplatin bind to in DNA?","Nitrogen atoms on two adjacent guanine bases."],["How are amino acids from a hydrolysed protein identified?","Thin-layer chromatography, comparing Rf values (spots located with ninhydrin or UV light)."],["Why does an enzyme act only on one enantiomer?","Its active site is stereospecific."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[{"q":"Which type of bond holds the α-helix together?","o":["Peptide bonds","Hydrogen bonds","Disulfide bonds","Ionic bonds"],"a":1,"e":"Hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H groups in the chain hold the helix."},{"q":"Which base pairs with cytosine in DNA?","o":["Adenine","Guanine","Thymine","Uracil"],"a":1,"e":"C–G pairs form three hydrogen bonds."},{"q":"How does cisplatin act as an anticancer drug?","o":["It breaks peptide bonds","It binds to guanine bases in DNA, preventing replication","It is an enzyme inhibitor in the liver","It oxidises cancer cells"],"a":1,"e":"Pt bonds to N atoms on adjacent guanines, distorting DNA so it cannot replicate."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Amino Acids, Proteins and DNA","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.13.%20Amino%20Acids,%20Proteins%20and%20DNA.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Amines, Amino Acids, Amides and Peptides","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.13.%20Amines,%20Amino%20Acids,%20Amides%20and%20Peptides.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Amino Acids, Proteins and DNA","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.13.%20Amino%20Acids,%20Proteins%20and%20DNA.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.14","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Organic synthesis","short":"Planning multi-step syntheses and choosing reagents and conditions.","spec":["Multi-step syntheses of up to four steps, with reagents and conditions for each step","Making new C–C bonds (cyanide ions, Friedel–Crafts acylation) to lengthen a carbon chain","Chemists aim to design processes that avoid hazardous solvents and use fewer steps with high atom economy","Practical techniques: reflux, distillation, separation, purification by recrystallisation, and melting point (required practical 10)"],"notes":"\n<h2>Practical techniques for organic solids</h2>\n<h3>Filtration under reduced pressure</h3>\n<p>Used to separate a solid product from a liquid quickly. A <strong>Büchner funnel</strong> with a filter paper fits into a side-arm (Büchner) flask connected to a water pump. Wet the paper with solvent first so it seals; pour in the mixture; the suction pulls the liquid through, leaving a fairly dry solid. Wash with a little cold solvent.</p>\n<h3>Recrystallisation</h3>\n<p>Purifies an impure solid. It relies on the product being <strong>much more soluble in the hot solvent than in the cold</strong>, while impurities either stay dissolved or don't dissolve at all.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Dissolve the impure solid in the <strong>minimum volume of hot solvent</strong> (so the solution is saturated and as much as possible crystallises on cooling).</li>\n<li>If there are insoluble impurities, filter the hot solution (using a pre-heated funnel).</li>\n<li>Let the solution <strong>cool slowly</strong>, then in ice. Crystals of the product form; soluble impurities stay in solution.</li>\n<li>Filter under reduced pressure, wash with a little <strong>ice-cold solvent</strong> (to remove the impure solution without dissolving much product).</li>\n<li>Dry the crystals (in air, in a warm oven, or a desiccator).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Melting point determination</h3>\n<p>Pack a little solid into a sealed capillary tube and heat slowly in a melting-point apparatus (or a Thiele tube of oil). A <strong>pure</strong> compound melts <strong>sharply</strong> at its literature value. <strong>Impurities lower the melting point and make it melt over a wider range.</strong> Compare with a data-book value to check identity and purity.</p>\n\n<h2>Recognising functional groups</h2>\n<p>Molecules in A level questions often have several functional groups (for example a drug molecule with an amine, an ester, an alcohol and a benzene ring). You need to identify each one and predict how the molecule reacts with a given reagent: each group reacts independently. A reagent such as NaOH(aq) might hydrolyse an ester and neutralise a phenol and a carboxylic acid in the same molecule.</p>\n\n<h2>Multi-stage synthesis</h2>\n<p>Synthesis questions give a starting compound and a target and ask for a route, with reagents and conditions for each step. Work <strong>forwards</strong> from the starting material and <strong>backwards</strong> from the target until they meet. Look at what has changed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Has the <strong>carbon chain got longer</strong>? You need a C–C bond: a nitrile (from a halogenoalkane or carbonyl), or Friedel–Crafts on a ring.</li>\n<li>Has a <strong>functional group changed</strong>? Find a sequence on the reaction map (e.g. alcohol → halogenoalkane → nitrile → amine).</li>\n<li>Is a <strong>group on a benzene ring</strong> involved? Use nitration, reduction to phenylamine, halogenation, Friedel–Crafts, and consider directing effects.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Suggest a three-step route from ethanol to propylamine (CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂).</p>\n<p>The chain goes from 2 to 3 carbons, and ends with an amine: think \"nitrile, then reduce\".</p>\n<p>1. Ethanol + NaBr and conc. H₂SO₄, heat → bromoethane.</p>\n<p>2. Bromoethane + NaCN in ethanol, heat → propanenitrile.</p>\n<p>3. Propanenitrile + H₂, Ni catalyst → <strong>propylamine</strong>.</p></div>\n<p>The full map of reactions, with reagents and conditions for every step, is on the <a href=\"#rxmap\">Reaction map</a> page.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> for each step, give reagent(s), conditions (heat, reflux, catalyst, solvent) and the structure of the intermediate. Marks are often lost for a missing \"conc.\" or \"under reflux\".</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Example preparation: aspirin</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Warm salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid) with ethanoic anhydride and a few drops of concentrated sulfuric or phosphoric acid catalyst; the phenol –OH is esterified.</li>\n<li>Add cold water to destroy excess anhydride and crystallise the product; cool in ice.</li>\n<li><strong>Filter under reduced pressure</strong> and wash with ice-cold water.</li>\n<li><strong>Recrystallise</strong> from a minimum of hot ethanol/water; cool; filter; dry.</li>\n<li>Weigh to find the yield; measure the <strong>melting point</strong> (aspirin about 136 °C).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Interpreting melting point results</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Two samples of a product melt at 132–135 °C and 135–136 °C. The literature value is 136 °C. Which is purer?</p>\n<p>The second: it melts over a narrow range close to the literature value. The first melts lower and over a wider range, showing impurities.</p></div>\n<h2>Multi-functional molecules</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A molecule contains a phenol group, a primary alcohol group and an ester group. Predict what happens with (a) NaOH(aq), cold; (b) NaOH(aq), heated under reflux; (c) acidified dichromate, reflux.</p>\n<p>(a) The phenol is neutralised (phenoxide). (b) As (a), and the ester is hydrolysed to a carboxylate and an alcohol. (c) The primary alcohol is oxidised to a carboxylic acid; the phenol and ester don't react.</p></div>\n<h2>Strategy for longer syntheses</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Write out the reaction map from memory before the exam.</li>\n<li>For aromatic targets, decide the order of substitutions: directing effects matter (–NO₂ directs to 3; –OH and –NH₂ to 2 and 4).</li>\n<li>Count carbons at every step.</li>\n<li>Check each step only changes what you intend: e.g. NaBH₄ reduces C=O in aldehydes and ketones but not in carboxylic acids or esters.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is the minimum volume of hot solvent used in recrystallisation?</summary><div><p>So the solution is saturated and as much product as possible crystallises out when it cools, maximising the yield.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why are the crystals washed with ice-cold solvent?</summary><div><p>To remove soluble impurities left on the surface without dissolving much of the product.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Suggest a two-step route from propan-1-ol to propyl propanoate.</summary><div><p>Oxidise some propan-1-ol with excess acidified dichromate under reflux to propanoic acid; then react the acid with more propan-1-ol and conc. H₂SO₄ catalyst.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Why form C–C bonds?</h2>\n<p>Many syntheses need a longer carbon chain than the starting material. Forming a new C–C bond is the way to <strong>increase the length of the carbon chain</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Nitriles</h2>\n<p>Nitriles contain the –C≡N group. Two ways to make them, each adding <strong>one carbon</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>From a halogenoalkane</strong>: heat with <strong>sodium cyanide (or potassium cyanide) in ethanol</strong>. Nucleophilic substitution, with CN⁻ as the nucleophile:<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CH₂Br + CN⁻ → CH₃CH₂CN + Br⁻ &nbsp;&nbsp;(propanenitrile)</div></li>\n<li><strong>From a carbonyl compound</strong>: <strong>HCN</strong> (made from NaCN and H₂SO₄) adds by nucleophilic addition, giving a hydroxynitrile:<div class=\"rxn\">CH₃CHO + HCN → CH₃CH(OH)CN</div></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The nitrile carbon counts in the name: CH₃CH₂CN is propanenitrile (3 carbons).</p>\n<h3>Reactions of nitriles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reduction to amines</strong>: H₂ with a <strong>nickel catalyst</strong> (or LiAlH₄): CH₃CH₂CN + 2H₂ → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ (propylamine).</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrolysis to carboxylic acids</strong>: heat under reflux with <strong>dilute aqueous acid</strong> (e.g. HCl): CH₃CH₂CN + 2H₂O + H⁺ → CH₃CH₂COOH + NH₄⁺.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Suggest a two-step synthesis of propanoic acid from bromoethane.</p>\n<p>Step 1: bromoethane + NaCN in ethanol, heat → propanenitrile (adds one carbon).</p>\n<p>Step 2: propanenitrile + dilute HCl, heat under reflux → <strong>propanoic acid</strong>.</p></div>\n\n<h2>Friedel–Crafts reactions</h2>\n<p>On aromatic rings, C–C bonds are formed by <strong>Friedel–Crafts alkylation</strong> (with a halogenoalkane) and <strong>acylation</strong> (with an acyl chloride), both with a <strong>halogen carrier</strong> such as AlCl₃ (see 6.1.1).</p>\n<div class=\"rxn\">C₆H₆ + CH₃Cl → C₆H₅CH₃ + HCl<br>C₆H₆ + CH₃COCl → C₆H₅COCH₃ + HCl</div>\n<p>Acylation is often preferred, because alkylation can continue to give several alkyl groups on the ring.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> the solvent matters for cyanide reactions: NaCN in <strong>ethanol</strong> for halogenoalkanes (water would give the alcohol). Don't confuse it with HCN for carbonyls.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Why chain length matters</h2>\n<p>Many useful molecules (drugs, flavours, polymers) need specific carbon skeletons. Most simple reactions keep the number of carbons the same. Cyanide is the key: CN⁻ is a carbon nucleophile, so the new bond formed is C–C.</p>\n<h2>Naming nitriles and hydroxynitriles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The C of –C≡N is carbon 1: CH₃CN is ethanenitrile; CH₃CH₂CH₂CN is butanenitrile.</li>\n<li>Hydroxynitriles: CH₃CH(OH)CN is 2-hydroxypropanenitrile (OH on carbon 2, next to the nitrile carbon).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Mechanism reminder</h2>\n<p>With a halogenoalkane, CN⁻ reacts by <strong>nucleophilic substitution</strong> (the lone pair on the carbon of CN⁻ attacks the δ+ carbon; C–X breaks). With a carbonyl, CN⁻ reacts by <strong>nucleophilic addition</strong> (attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon; then O⁻ takes H⁺ from HCN or H⁺).</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Suggest a route from ethanal to 2-hydroxypropanoic acid (lactic acid).</p>\n<p>1. HCN (NaCN / H₂SO₄) → 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, CH₃CH(OH)CN.</p>\n<p>2. Dilute HCl, heat under reflux → <strong>CH₃CH(OH)COOH</strong>. (The product is racemic.)</p></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Suggest a route from benzene to 1-phenylethanol, C₆H₅CH(OH)CH₃.</p>\n<p>1. Ethanoyl chloride + AlCl₃ (Friedel–Crafts acylation) → phenylethanone, C₆H₅COCH₃.</p>\n<p>2. NaBH₄ → <strong>1-phenylethanol</strong>.</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Name the product of 1-bromobutane with NaCN in ethanol.</summary><div><p>Pentanenitrile, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CN.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What is formed when propanenitrile is reduced with H₂ and a nickel catalyst?</summary><div><p>Propylamine, CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is acylation often preferred to alkylation in Friedel–Crafts reactions?</summary><div><p>The alkylbenzene product is more reactive than benzene and can be alkylated again (multiple substitution); acylation stops after one substitution.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Choosing the right C–C method</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Starting material</th><th>Reagent</th><th>Product</th><th>Then</th></tr>\n<tr><td>halogenoalkane</td><td>NaCN in ethanol</td><td>nitrile (+1 C)</td><td>reduce → amine, or hydrolyse → acid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>aldehyde or ketone</td><td>NaCN / H₂SO₄</td><td>hydroxynitrile (+1 C)</td><td>hydrolyse → 2-hydroxy acid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>benzene</td><td>RCl / AlCl₃</td><td>alkylbenzene</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>benzene</td><td>RCOCl / AlCl₃</td><td>aromatic ketone</td><td>NaBH₄ → secondary alcohol</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>More to try</h2><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Suggest a two-step route from chloromethane to ethylamine.</summary><div><p>1. NaCN in ethanol, heat → ethanenitrile CH₃CN. 2. H₂ with a Ni catalyst → ethylamine CH₃CH₂NH₂.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Practical techniques in organic synthesis</h2>\n<h3>Heating under reflux</h3>\n<p>Organic reactions are often slow, and organic liquids are volatile and flammable. <strong>Reflux</strong> lets you heat a reaction mixture for a long time without boiling it dry or losing product.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pear-shaped or round-bottomed flask, no more than half full, with <strong>anti-bumping granules</strong> (smooth boiling).</li>\n<li>A <strong>Liebig condenser</strong> fitted vertically; water goes in at the <strong>bottom</strong> and out at the top so the condenser stays full of cold water.</li>\n<li>Heat with an electric heating mantle or water bath (no naked flame). The top of the condenser is <strong>open</strong>: a sealed system would build up pressure.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Distillation</h3>\n<p>Separates a liquid from a mixture by its boiling point. The flask is fitted with a still head and thermometer (bulb level with the side arm, so it reads the temperature of the vapour leaving), and a sloping condenser leading to a collecting flask. Collect the liquid that distils within a degree or two of the product's boiling point.</p>\n\n<h2>Purifying an organic liquid</h2>\n<p>A crude product usually contains unreacted reactants, by-products, water and acid. A typical sequence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Separating funnel</strong>: add the crude product and an aqueous solution, shake (releasing pressure through the tap), let the layers separate, and run off the bottom layer. Keep the organic layer. Which layer is which depends on density: most organic liquids float, but halogenated ones sink. Adding water and checking which layer gets bigger tells you.</li>\n<li><strong>Washing</strong>: shake with <strong>sodium hydrogencarbonate solution</strong> to remove acid impurities (CO₂ is released, so vent the funnel often), then with water.</li>\n<li><strong>Drying</strong>: add an anhydrous salt (<strong>anhydrous MgSO₄ or CaCl₂</strong>), swirl, and leave. The liquid is dry when it goes from cloudy to <strong>clear</strong> and fresh solid no longer clumps. Decant or filter off the solid.</li>\n<li><strong>Redistillation</strong>: distil again and collect the fraction at the product's boiling point. A narrow boiling range shows a pure product.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>Identifying functional groups from reactions</h2>\n<p>You need to be able to work out a functional group from the reagents it reacts with and the products formed, using everything in modules 4.1–4.2: an orange-to-green dichromate colour change means a primary or secondary alcohol (or aldehyde); decolourising bromine water means C=C; a precipitate with AgNO₃ in ethanol means a halogenoalkane.</p>\n\n<h2>Synthetic routes</h2>\n<p>Synthesis questions ask how to turn one compound into another in two steps. Learn the reactions as a <strong>map</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Reagents and conditions</th></tr>\n<tr><td>alkane</td><td>halogenoalkane</td><td>X₂, UV light (radical substitution)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alkene</td><td>alkane</td><td>H₂, Ni catalyst, 150 °C</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alkene</td><td>dihalogenoalkane</td><td>X₂, room temperature</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alkene</td><td>halogenoalkane</td><td>HX, room temperature</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alkene</td><td>alcohol</td><td>H₂O(g), H₃PO₄ catalyst</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alcohol</td><td>alkene</td><td>conc. H₃PO₄ or H₂SO₄, heat (elimination)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alcohol</td><td>halogenoalkane</td><td>NaX + conc. H₂SO₄, heat</td></tr>\n<tr><td>halogenoalkane</td><td>alcohol</td><td>NaOH(aq), heat under reflux</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1° alcohol</td><td>aldehyde</td><td>K₂Cr₂O₇ / H₂SO₄, distil</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1° alcohol</td><td>carboxylic acid</td><td>excess K₂Cr₂O₇ / H₂SO₄, reflux</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2° alcohol</td><td>ketone</td><td>K₂Cr₂O₇ / H₂SO₄, reflux</td></tr></table></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Suggest a two-step synthesis of propanone from propene.</p>\n<p>Step 1: propene + steam with an H₃PO₄ catalyst → propan-2-ol (Markownikoff: OH goes on the middle carbon as the major product).</p>\n<p>Step 2: propan-2-ol + acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, heat under reflux → <strong>propanone</strong>.</p></div>\n<p>The full interactive version is on the <a href=\"#rxmap\">Reaction map</a> page.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> always give the reagent, the conditions and, where it matters, the type of reaction. \"NaOH\" alone is not enough: \"NaOH(aq), heat under reflux, nucleophilic substitution\".</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Example preparation: 1-bromobutane</h2>\n<p>A typical aliphatic preparation combines all the techniques in this topic.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Put butan-1-ol, sodium bromide, water and concentrated sulfuric acid (added slowly, with cooling) in a round-bottomed flask with anti-bumping granules.</li>\n<li><strong>Heat under reflux</strong> for about 45 minutes: H₂SO₄ + NaBr make HBr, which substitutes the –OH.</li>\n<li>Rearrange for <strong>distillation</strong> and distil off the crude 1-bromobutane with water.</li>\n<li>Transfer to a <strong>separating funnel</strong>. 1-bromobutane is denser than water, so it is the <strong>lower</strong> layer. Run it off.</li>\n<li>Wash with <strong>sodium hydrogencarbonate solution</strong> to neutralise acid, releasing pressure often; then with water.</li>\n<li><strong>Dry</strong> with anhydrous calcium chloride until the liquid is clear.</li>\n<li><strong>Redistil</strong>, collecting the fraction boiling at 100–104 °C (b.p. 101 °C).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Calculating yield in a preparation</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> 7.40 g of butan-1-ol (M = 74.0) gives 8.22 g of 1-bromobutane (M = 137.0). Find the percentage yield.</p>\n<p>n(butan-1-ol) = 0.100 mol; theoretical n(product) = 0.100 mol, mass 13.70 g.</p>\n$$\\%\\ \\text{yield}=\\dfrac{8.22}{13.70}\\times 100=\\mathbf{60.0\\,\\%}$$<p>Losses: incomplete reaction, side reactions (elimination to but-1-ene), and product left behind during transfers and separation.</p></div>\n<h2>Two-step routes: more examples</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Ethene to ethanoic acid.</p>\n<p>1. Steam, H₃PO₄ catalyst → ethanol. 2. Excess acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, heat under reflux → <strong>ethanoic acid</strong>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Propan-1-ol to 1,2-dibromopropane.</p>\n<p>1. Conc. H₃PO₄, heat → propene (elimination). 2. Br₂ at room temperature → <strong>1,2-dibromopropane</strong> (electrophilic addition).</p></div>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is the top of a reflux condenser left open?</summary><div><p>To stop pressure building up as the mixture is heated, which could make the apparatus burst.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How can you tell which layer in a separating funnel is aqueous?</summary><div><p>Add a little water and see which layer increases in volume (or check densities).</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How do you know when an organic liquid is dry?</summary><div><p>It changes from cloudy to clear, and fresh drying agent no longer clumps together.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Designing a synthesis (AQA)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Work backwards from the target molecule, and forwards from the starting material, to find the functional group changes needed. AQA syntheses usually have up to four steps.</li>\n<li>For each step give the <strong>reagent</strong>, the <strong>conditions</strong> (catalyst, temperature, reflux or distil, solvent) and the <strong>name or structure of the intermediate</strong>.</li>\n<li>Good processes have fewer steps, a high atom economy, high yields, and avoid hazardous solvents and reagents.</li>\n<li>To add one carbon atom, use KCN: halogenoalkane → nitrile (then reduce to an amine or hydrolyse to a carboxylic acid), or carbonyl → hydroxynitrile.</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Nitrile","A compound containing the –C≡N functional group."]],"qa":[["Melting point determination for purity","Pure solids melt sharply at the data-book value; impurities lower and broaden the range."],["Recrystallisation steps","Dissolve in minimum hot solvent, filter hot if needed, cool to crystallise, filter under reduced pressure, wash with cold solvent, dry."],["Why use the minimum volume of hot solvent?","So as much product as possible crystallises on cooling."],["Filtration under reduced pressure","Büchner flask and funnel connected to a vacuum: fast separation of crystals."],["Why wash crystals with cold solvent?","Removes soluble impurities without dissolving much product."],["Reagents: alcohol → alkene","Concentrated H₂SO₄ or H₃PO₄, heat (elimination)."],["Reagents: nitrile → carboxylic acid","Dilute HCl, heat under reflux."],["Reagents: nitrobenzene → phenylamine","Tin and concentrated HCl, heat under reflux, then NaOH."],["Reagents: aldehyde → primary alcohol","NaBH₄ (aq)."],["Reagents: carboxylic acid → acyl chloride","SOCl₂."],["How do you plan a multi-stage synthesis?","Identify the functional groups in the start and target molecules; work out intermediates; give reagents and conditions for each step."],["Halogenoalkane + CN⁻: conditions and product","NaCN or KCN in ethanol, heat: nitrile with one more carbon."],["Reducing a nitrile","H₂ with Ni catalyst: amine."],["Hydrolysing a nitrile","Heat with dilute acid (HCl): carboxylic acid."],["Why is C–C bond formation important in synthesis?","It increases the length of the carbon chain."],["Friedel–Crafts: which bond does it form?","A C–C bond to the benzene ring (alkylation or acylation)."],["Mechanism of halogenoalkane + CN⁻","Nucleophilic substitution: the cyanide lone pair attacks the δ+ carbon."],["Carbonyl + HCN: what is formed and how many carbons are added?","A hydroxynitrile; one extra carbon."],["Hydroxynitrile from an aldehyde: is the product chiral?","Usually yes: it forms a racemic mixture, since CN⁻ attacks the planar C=O from either side."],["Functional group made by Friedel–Crafts acylation","A ketone attached to the ring (an aromatic ketone)."],["Why heat under reflux?","To heat a volatile reaction mixture for a long time without losing reactants or products by evaporation."],["Why is a Liebig condenser fed with water from the bottom?","So it fills completely and cools most efficiently."],["Why must a reflux or distillation apparatus not be sealed?","Pressure would build up as it is heated."],["Why add anti-bumping granules?","To give smooth boiling and avoid large bubbles."],["Steps to purify an organic liquid","Separating funnel to remove the aqueous layer; wash (e.g. NaHCO₃ to remove acid); dry with anhydrous salt; redistil, collecting at the boiling point."],["Common drying agents","Anhydrous MgSO₄, CaCl₂ or Na₂SO₄: the liquid goes from cloudy to clear."],["How do you find which layer is aqueous in a separating funnel?","Add some water and see which layer increases in volume."],["Reagents: alkane → halogenoalkane","Halogen and UV light (radical substitution)."],["Reagents: halogenoalkane → alcohol","NaOH(aq), heat under reflux."],["Reagents: alkene → dihalogenoalkane","Halogen at room temperature (electrophilic addition)."]],"eqs":[],"mcq":[],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Organic Synthesis","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.14.%20Organic%20Synthesis.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Organic Synthesis - Extending Carbon Chains, Friedel-Crafts Reactions and Chiral Synthesis","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.14.%20Organic%20Synthesis%20-%20Extending%20Carbon%20Chains,%20Friedel-Crafts%20Reactions%20and%20Chiral%20Synthesis.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Organic Synthesis","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.14.%20Organic%20Synthesis.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.15","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy","short":"¹³C and ¹H NMR: chemical shift, integration and splitting.","spec":["Nuclear magnetic resonance and chemical shift relative to TMS (tetramethylsilane)","¹³C NMR: number of carbon environments and their chemical shifts","¹H NMR: number of environments, relative intensity (integration) and chemical shift","Spin–spin splitting with the n + 1 rule: doublets, triplets and quartets","Solvents such as CDCl₃ and CCl₄ that contain no ¹H","Combining NMR with other evidence to deduce structures"],"notes":"\n<h2>NMR spectroscopy</h2>\n<p>Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) detects nuclei such as ¹H and ¹³C in a strong magnetic field. Atoms in different <strong>chemical environments</strong> (bonded to different groups) absorb at different frequencies, recorded as the <strong>chemical shift, δ</strong>, in <strong>ppm</strong> (parts per million).</p>\n<h3>Practical points</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TMS</strong>, tetramethylsilane Si(CH₃)₄, is the <strong>reference standard</strong>, δ = 0. It is used because all 12 H (and 4 C) are in the same environment (one sharp peak), it absorbs outside the range of most organic compounds, and it is inert, non-toxic and volatile (easy to remove).</li>\n<li>Samples are dissolved in a <strong>deuterated solvent</strong> such as CDCl₃. Deuterium (²H) doesn't produce a ¹H NMR signal, so the solvent doesn't add peaks.</li>\n<li>Peaks from <strong>O–H and N–H</strong> protons can be identified with <strong>D₂O</strong>: shake the sample with D₂O; the H on O or N exchanges with D (–OH → –OD), and that peak <strong>disappears</strong> from the spectrum.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>¹³C NMR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>number of peaks</strong> = the number of different <strong>carbon environments</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>chemical shift</strong> of each peak tells you the type of environment (look it up on AQA Data Booklet): C–C about 5–55, C–O and C–N 30–70 (C–O about 50–70), C=C and aromatic 100–150, C=O 160–220 ppm.</li>\n<li>Peaks aren't split and peak heights don't matter at A level.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Symmetry reduces the number of environments: propanone CH₃COCH₃ has 2 carbon environments (two equivalent CH₃ and one C=O); benzene has 1; methylbenzene has 5 (CH₃ and four ring environments).</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> How many peaks in the ¹³C NMR spectra of propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol?</p>\n<p>Propan-1-ol CH₃CH₂CH₂OH: all three carbons different → <strong>3 peaks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Propan-2-ol CH₃CH(OH)CH₃: the two CH₃ are equivalent → <strong>2 peaks</strong>. So ¹³C NMR tells these isomers apart.</p></div>\n\n<h2>¹H (proton) NMR</h2>\n<p>A ¹H spectrum gives four pieces of information:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Number of peaks</strong> = number of different proton environments.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemical shift</strong> = type of environment (AQA Data Booklet): R–CH 0.5–2.0; H–C–C=O 2.0–3.0; H–C–O and H–C–N 3.0–4.3; aromatic H 6.5–8.0; aldehyde CHO 9.5–10.0; carboxylic acid COOH 10–12; alcohol O–H 0.5–5 (variable).</li>\n<li><strong>Relative peak area</strong> (shown by an integration trace or ratio numbers) = <strong>relative number of protons</strong> in each environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Splitting pattern</strong> = number of protons on <strong>adjacent carbons</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Spin–spin splitting: the n + 1 rule</h3>\n<p>The signal for a set of protons is split by the protons on the <strong>neighbouring carbon(s)</strong>. If there are <strong>n</strong> equivalent H on adjacent carbons, the peak splits into <strong>n + 1</strong> lines.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Neighbouring H (n)</th><th>Peaks (n + 1)</th><th>Name</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>singlet</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>doublet</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>triplet</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>quartet</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Protons on O or N usually appear as <strong>singlets</strong> and don't split their neighbours (they exchange rapidly). Equivalent protons don't split each other.</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Predict the ¹H NMR spectrum of ethanol, CH₃CH₂OH.</p>\n<p>Three environments: CH₃, CH₂, OH, in the ratio <strong>3 : 2 : 1</strong>.</p>\n<p>CH₃ (δ ≈ 1.2): next to CH₂ (2 H) → <strong>triplet</strong>. CH₂ (δ ≈ 3.7, next to O): next to CH₃ (3 H) → <strong>quartet</strong>. OH (δ ≈ 2–5): <strong>singlet</strong>, which disappears with D₂O.</p>\n<p>A triplet plus quartet pattern is the signature of an <strong>ethyl group</strong>, CH₃CH₂–.</p></div>\n\n<h2>Combining techniques</h2>\n<p>Structure-determination questions give several pieces of evidence. Work through them in a fixed order and write down what each one shows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Elemental analysis</strong> → empirical formula.</li>\n<li><strong>Mass spectrum</strong>: molecular ion peak → M<sub>r</sub> → molecular formula; fragments → parts of the molecule.</li>\n<li><strong>IR</strong> → functional groups (O–H, C=O, etc.).</li>\n<li><strong>¹³C NMR</strong> → number and types of carbon environment.</li>\n<li><strong>¹H NMR</strong> → proton environments, their ratio, neighbours (splitting), and which are O–H/N–H (D₂O).</li>\n<li>Propose a structure and check it against <strong>every</strong> piece of data.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A compound C₄H₈O₂ has an IR peak at 1740 cm⁻¹ and no broad O–H. Its ¹H NMR shows: δ 1.2 (3H, triplet), δ 2.0 (3H, singlet), δ 4.1 (2H, quartet). Identify it.</p>\n<p>C=O, no O–H, two oxygens: an <strong>ester</strong>. Triplet (3H) + quartet (2H): an ethyl group; the quartet at δ 4.1 means the CH₂ is bonded to O (–O–CH₂CH₃).</p>\n<p>Singlet (3H) at δ 2.0: a CH₃ next to C=O with no neighbouring H.</p>\n<p>The compound is <strong>ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃</strong>.</p></div>\n<p>Practise with the problems on the <a href=\"#spec\">Spectroscopy practice</a> page.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> for each ¹H peak, write a line: \"δ 4.1, 2H, quartet: CH₂ bonded to O, next to a CH₃\". Examiners award marks for each piece of evidence linked to a feature of the structure.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>How many peaks would you expect in the ¹H NMR spectrum of propanone, and what splitting?</summary><div><p>One peak (6H), a singlet: both CH₃ groups are equivalent and have no neighbouring H.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>What splitting pattern does the CH₂ in CH₃CH₂Cl show?</summary><div><p>A quartet (three H on the neighbouring CH₃).</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is CDCl₃ used as the solvent?</summary><div><p>Deuterium does not give a ¹H NMR signal, so the solvent adds no peaks to the spectrum.</p></div></details>\n<h2>AQA NMR details</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Chemical shifts are measured relative to <strong>TMS</strong>, Si(CH₃)₄, given δ = 0. TMS is used because it is inert, non-toxic, volatile (easily removed) and gives a single strong peak upfield of almost everything else, because all 12 protons are equivalent.</li>\n<li>Samples are dissolved in solvents with no ¹H atoms, such as <strong>CDCl₃</strong> or CCl₄, so the solvent gives no peak in the ¹H spectrum.</li>\n<li>Use Tables B and C in the AQA Data Booklet for chemical shift ranges.</li>\n<li>The integration trace gives the relative number of protons in each environment.</li>\n</ul>\n","terms":[["Chemical shift (δ)","The position of an NMR peak, in ppm, relative to TMS; depends on the chemical environment."],["TMS","Tetramethylsilane, Si(CH₃)₄: the reference at δ = 0 ppm (12 equivalent protons, inert, volatile)."]],"qa":[["Why use deuterated solvents (e.g. CDCl₃) in NMR?","They contain no ¹H, so they do not give a peak."],["What does the number of peaks in ¹³C NMR show?","The number of different carbon environments."],["What does the relative peak area show in ¹H NMR?","The ratio of the number of protons in each environment."],["Splitting: singlet, doublet, triplet, quartet","0, 1, 2 and 3 H on adjacent carbons (n + 1 rule)."],["How do you identify an O–H or N–H peak?","Add D₂O: the peak disappears as H is replaced by D."],["Typical ¹³C shift of C=O","About 160–220 ppm."],["Order to combine evidence for a structure","Elemental analysis → empirical formula; mass spec → Mr; IR → functional groups; NMR → environments and connectivity."]],"eqs":[["n neighbouring H → n + 1 peaks","Spin–spin splitting rule"]],"mcq":[{"q":"Why is CDCl₃ used as a solvent for ¹H NMR?","o":["It is chiral","It has no ¹H atoms, so gives no peak","It dissolves TMS only","It reacts with the sample"],"a":1,"e":"Deuterium does not give a ¹H signal."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":[],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.15.%20Nuclear%20Magnetic%20Resonance%20Spectroscopy.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: NMR Spectroscopy","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.15.%20NMR%20Spectroscopy.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Nuclear magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.15.%20Nuclear%20magnetic%20Resonance%20Spectroscopy.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"}]});
S.addTopic({"id":"3.3.16","mod":3,"as":false,"title":"Chromatography","short":"TLC, column chromatography and gas chromatography, with Rf values and retention times.","spec":["Chromatography separates components by their balance between a stationary phase and a mobile phase","Thin-layer chromatography (TLC) and Rf values (required practical 12)","Column chromatography","Gas chromatography: retention times, peak areas, and GC–MS"],"notes":"\n<h2>Chromatography</h2>\n<p>Chromatography separates the components of a mixture. Every method has a <strong>stationary phase</strong> (which stays still) and a <strong>mobile phase</strong> (which moves through it). Components separate because they have different affinities for the two phases: each spends a different amount of time <strong>adsorbed</strong> onto the stationary phase versus dissolved in the mobile phase, so they travel at different speeds.</p>\n<h3>Thin-layer chromatography (TLC)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stationary phase</strong>: a thin layer of silica (SiO₂) or alumina (Al₂O₃) on a glass or plastic plate.</li>\n<li><strong>Mobile phase</strong>: a solvent (or mixture).</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw a pencil baseline (pencil doesn't dissolve) and spot the samples on it.</li>\n<li>Stand the plate in a little solvent in a covered beaker, with the solvent below the baseline (so the samples don't dissolve into the pool).</li>\n<li>Remove the plate when the solvent is near the top; mark the <strong>solvent front</strong> immediately.</li>\n<li>Locate colourless spots under <strong>UV light</strong> or with <strong>iodine vapour</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"eqgrid\"><div>$$R_f=\\dfrac{\\text{distance moved by spot}}{\\text{distance moved by solvent front}}$$</div></div>\n<p>A substance's R<sub>f</sub> is fixed for a given stationary phase and solvent, so it can be compared with known substances run on the same plate. A pure substance gives a single spot. More polar compounds are adsorbed more strongly onto polar silica, so they move less and have lower R<sub>f</sub>.</p>\n<h3>Gas chromatography (GC)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stationary phase</strong>: a high-boiling liquid coated on a solid support, inside a long coiled column in an oven.</li>\n<li><strong>Mobile phase</strong>: an unreactive carrier gas (helium or nitrogen).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>retention time</strong> is the time from injection to detection: each compound has its own, so it identifies the compound (by comparison with a known sample under the same conditions). The <strong>area under each peak</strong> is proportional to the amount of that component, so GC also gives the relative composition. GC can be linked to a mass spectrometer (GC–MS) to identify each component, used in forensics, airport security and drug testing.</p>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A GC trace has two peaks with areas 30 and 90 units. What is the percentage composition?</p>\n$$\\text{first component}=\\dfrac{30}{120}\\times 100=\\mathbf{25\\,\\%}$$$$\\text{second}=\\dfrac{90}{120}\\times 100=\\mathbf{75\\,\\%}$$</div>\n\n<h2>Tests for organic functional groups</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Group</th><th>Test</th><th>Positive result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>alkene C=C</td><td>bromine water, shake</td><td>orange → colourless</td></tr>\n<tr><td>halogenoalkane</td><td>warm with AgNO₃(aq) in ethanol</td><td>precipitate: white (Cl), cream (Br), yellow (I)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>carbonyl (aldehyde or ketone)</td><td>2,4-DNP</td><td>orange precipitate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>aldehyde</td><td>Tollens' reagent, warm</td><td>silver mirror</td></tr>\n<tr><td>primary or secondary alcohol, aldehyde</td><td>acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, warm</td><td>orange → green</td></tr>\n<tr><td>carboxylic acid</td><td>Na₂CO₃(aq) or NaHCO₃(aq)</td><td>effervescence; CO₂ turns limewater milky</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote class=\"tip\"><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> R<sub>f</sub> values have no units and are always less than 1. Measure both distances from the <strong>baseline</strong>, to the centre of the spot.</p></blockquote>\n\n<h2>Why components separate</h2>\n<p>In TLC the silica stationary phase is polar (it has –OH groups on its surface). A polar compound adsorbs more strongly, spends more time stuck to the silica, and moves a shorter distance: low R<sub>f</sub>. A non-polar compound spends more time dissolved in a non-polar solvent and moves further: high R<sub>f</sub>. Changing the solvent changes all the R<sub>f</sub> values.</p>\n<h2>Using TLC in practice</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Checking purity</strong>: a pure product gives one spot.</li>\n<li><strong>Following a reaction</strong>: spot the starting material, the reaction mixture and a co-spot; when the starting-material spot disappears from the mixture lane, the reaction is complete.</li>\n<li><strong>Identifying a compound</strong>: compare with a known sample run on the same plate (same R<sub>f</sub>).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> In a TLC experiment the solvent front moved 8.0 cm. Spot A moved 2.4 cm and spot B 6.0 cm. Find their R<sub>f</sub> values and say which is more polar.</p>\n$$R_f(\\mathrm{A})=\\dfrac{2.4}{8.0}=\\mathbf{0.30}$$$$ R_f(\\mathrm{B})=\\dfrac{6.0}{8.0}=\\mathbf{0.75}$$<p>A is more polar (more strongly adsorbed on silica).</p></div>\n<h2>Gas chromatography: quantitative use</h2>\n<div class=\"wex\"><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A GC of a mixture of ethanol, propan-1-ol and butan-1-ol shows peak areas 1.2, 2.4 and 0.4 cm². Find the percentage of propan-1-ol.</p>\n$$\\%\\ \\text{propan-1-ol}=\\dfrac{2.4}{1.2+2.4+0.4}\\times 100=\\mathbf{60\\,\\%}$$\n<p>To identify which peak is which, inject each pure compound separately under the same conditions and compare retention times (or use GC–MS).</p></div>\n<p>Limitations: similar compounds may have very similar retention times; unknown compounds may have no reference to compare against. GC–MS solves this by giving a mass spectrum for each peak.</p>\n<h2>Check yourself</h2><p class=\"muted\">Try each one before opening the answer.</p><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Why is the baseline drawn in pencil, not ink?</summary><div><p>Ink would dissolve in the solvent and move up the plate, giving extra spots.</p></div></details><details class=\"fold\"><summary>Name the stationary and mobile phases in gas chromatography.</summary><div><p>Stationary: a high-boiling liquid on a solid support in the column. Mobile: an inert carrier gas such as helium or nitrogen.</p></div></details>","terms":[["Stationary phase","The phase that does not move in chromatography (solid in TLC, liquid on a solid support in GC)."],["Mobile phase","The phase that moves through the stationary phase (solvent in TLC, inert carrier gas in GC)."],["Retention time","The time from injection of a sample to its detection in gas chromatography."]],"qa":[["How does TLC separate components?","By relative adsorption onto the stationary phase."],["How does GC separate components?","By relative solubility in the liquid stationary phase."],["Limitations of TLC and GC","Similar compounds may have similar Rf or retention times; not all compounds have reference data."],["Test for an alkene","Bromine water: orange to colourless."],["Test for a carboxylic acid","Na₂CO₃ or NaHCO₃: effervescence (CO₂)."],["Test for a primary or secondary alcohol, or aldehyde","Acidified dichromate: orange to green."],["Test for a halogenoalkane","Warm with aqueous AgNO₃ and ethanol: precipitate colour shows the halogen."]],"eqs":[["$R_f=\\dfrac{\\text{distance moved by spot}}{\\text{distance moved by solvent front}}$","Thin-layer chromatography"]],"mcq":[{"q":"On a TLC plate a spot moves 3.0 cm and the solvent front moves 7.5 cm. What is Rf?","o":["0.25","0.40","2.5","4.5"],"a":1,"e":"Rf = 3.0 ÷ 7.5 = 0.40."}],"exam":[],"labs":[],"pags":["rp12"],"packs":[{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 questions","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20QP.pdf"},{"set":"Topic questions","label":"Organic Chemistry 2 mark scheme","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Topic-Qs/AQA/Organic-II/OUP-Chapterwide/Organic%20Chemistry%202%20MS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"PMT notes: Chromatography","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Detailed/3.16.%20Chromatography.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Knockhardy notes: Chromatography and GCMS","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Knockhardy/3.16.%20Chromatography%20and%20GCMS.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Mind map: Chromatography","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Mind-Maps/3.16.%20Chromatography.pdf"},{"set":"Revision notes","label":"Definitions: Organic Chemistry 2","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Organic-II/Definitions%20-%20Organic%20Chemistry%20II%20-%20AQA%20Chemistry%20A-level.pdf"},{"set":"Required practical guides","label":"RP 12: Thin-layer chromatography (TLC)","url":"https://pmt.physicsandmathstutor.com/download/Chemistry/A-level/Notes/AQA/Practical-Skills/RP%2012%20-%20Thin-layer%20chromatography%20(TLC).pdf"}]});
})();
